window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert12-chem-amines","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"Amines · Practice","chapter":"Amines","subject":"Chemistry","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":1,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (reaction) with List-II (amine formed).\nList-I\n(A) Propanamide + $Br_2$/NaOH\n(B) Potassium phthalimide + $CH_3Br$, then alkaline hydrolysis\n(C) Butanenitrile + $LiAlH_4$\n(D) Propanamide + $LiAlH_4$\nList-II\n(i) Butan-1-amine\n(ii) Ethanamine\n(iii) Propan-1-amine\n(iv) Methanamine","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Hoffmann degradation removes one carbon. Propanamide (3 C) gives ethanamine (2 C), so A-(ii).\n(B) Gabriel synthesis transfers the alkyl group of the halide unchanged. CH$_3$Br gives methanamine, so B-(iv).\n(C) LiAlH$_4$ reduces the nitrile without loss of carbon. CH$_3$CH$_2$CH$_2$CN gives butan-1-amine, so C-(i).\n(D) LiAlH$_4$ reduces the amide with the same carbon count. Propanamide gives propan-1-amine, so D-(iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":2,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Ammonolysis of an alkyl halide to give an amine is normally carried out by heating the halide with:","image":"","options":["Aqueous ammonia in an open flask at 273 K","Liquid ammonia in the presence of Cu(I) at room temperature","An ethanolic solution of ammonia in a sealed tube at 373 K","Ammonium chloride and NaOH at 453–473 K"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In ammonolysis, ammonia acts as a nucleophile and cleaves the C–X bond of an alkyl or benzyl halide.\nNCERT states that an ethanolic solution of NH$_3$ is used and the reaction is carried out in a sealed tube at 373 K.\nThe tube is sealed because ammonia is volatile and would escape on heating.\nNone of the other sets of conditions is used for this reaction.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":3,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"The boiling points of four compounds of nearly equal molar mass are: $n$-butylamine 350.8 K, diethylamine 329.3 K, N,N-dimethylethanamine 310.5 K and 2-methylbutane 300.8 K. Which of the following interpretations of these data is correct?","image":"","options":["N,N-Dimethylethanamine boils lower than 2-methylbutane because it cannot form hydrogen bonds","The 21.5 K gap between $n$-butylamine and diethylamine arises because the primary amine has two N–H hydrogens available for intermolecular hydrogen bonding","N,N-Dimethylethanamine boils above 2-methylbutane because it forms intermolecular N–H···N hydrogen bonds","The boiling points fall from $n$-butylamine to N,N-dimethylethanamine mainly because molar mass decreases in that order"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Wrong: the tertiary amine (310.5 K) actually boils above the alkane (300.8 K), because the polar amine shows stronger intermolecular forces than the non-polar alkane.\n(2) Correct: 350.8 − 329.3 = 21.5 K. The primary amine has two N–H hydrogens, so its association is greater.\n(3) Wrong: a tertiary amine has no N–H bond.\n(4) Wrong: all three amines have a molar mass of 73 g/mol, so the trend comes from hydrogen bonding.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":4,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following reagent sequences will convert 3-methylaniline into 3-nitrotoluene?","image":"","options":["(i) $NaNO_2$/HCl at 273–278 K; (ii) $HBF_4$; (iii) aqueous $NaNO_2$, Cu, heat","Conc. $HNO_3$/conc. $H_2SO_4$ directly at room temperature","(i) $NaNO_2$/HCl at 273–278 K; (ii) $H_3PO_2$, $H_2O$","(i) $(CH_3CO)_2O$; (ii) conc. $HNO_3$/conc. $H_2SO_4$; (iii) hydrolysis by $H^+$ or $OH^-$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Target: the –NH$_2$ group of 3-methylaniline must be replaced by –NO$_2$ at the same carbon, with no other change to the ring.\nOption (1): Diazotisation gives 3-methylbenzenediazonium chloride. $HBF_4$ then precipitates the diazonium fluoroborate. Heating this with aqueous $NaNO_2$ and Cu replaces the diazo group by –NO$_2$, giving 3-nitrotoluene. Correct.\nOption (2): Direct nitration keeps the –NH$_2$ group and also gives tarry oxidation products.\nOption (3): $H_3PO_2$ replaces –N$_2^+$ by H, so the product is toluene.\nOption (4): Acetylation protects –NH$_2$, but hydrolysis brings the amino group back, giving a nitro-methylaniline.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":5,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"1.18 g of propan-1-amine (C3H9N, molar mass 59 g/mol) is treated with excess nitrous acid. Nitrogen is evolved quantitatively. What volume of N2 is liberated at STP? (Molar volume at STP = 22.4 L/mol)","image":"","options":["224 mL","448 mL","896 mL","44.8 mL"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: mass of amine = 1.18 g; molar mass = 59 g/mol.\nFormula: RNH2 + HNO2 → ROH + N2 + H2O, so 1 mol of amine gives 1 mol of N2.\nSubstituting: moles of amine = 1.18/59 = 0.02 mol, so N2 formed = 0.02 mol.\nVolume = 0.02 × 22400 mL = 448 mL.\nSo the volume of N2 at STP = 448 mL.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":6,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"What is the correct decreasing order of basic strength in the gas phase?","image":"","options":["$(C_2H_5)_2NH > (C_2H_5)_3N > C_2H_5NH_2 > NH_3$","$(C_2H_5)_3N > (C_2H_5)_2NH > C_2H_5NH_2 > NH_3$","$C_2H_5NH_2 > (C_2H_5)_2NH > (C_2H_5)_3N > NH_3$","$NH_3 > C_2H_5NH_2 > (C_2H_5)_2NH > (C_2H_5)_3N$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In the gas phase there is no solvation, so only the +I effect of the alkyl groups matters.\nMore alkyl groups push more electron density onto nitrogen and better stabilise the cation formed.\nThis gives the order 3° > 2° > 1° > NH$_3$.\nOption (1) is the aqueous-phase order for ethylamines, which is a common trap.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":7,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The product formed when ethanamine reacts with benzenesulphonyl chloride dissolves in aqueous alkali.\nReason (R): The hydrogen attached to nitrogen in N-ethylbenzenesulphonamide is strongly acidic because of the electron-withdrawing sulphonyl group.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Ethanamine, a primary amine, gives N-ethylbenzenesulphonamide, which is soluble in alkali.\nR is true. The –SO2– group strongly withdraws electrons, so the remaining N–H becomes acidic.\nThis acidic hydrogen is removed by the alkali, and the resulting salt dissolves. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":8,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (reaction) with List-II (main organic product).\nList-I\n(a) CH$_3$CH$_2$C≡N, reduced with LiAlH$_4$\n(b) CH$_3$CONHCH$_3$, reduced with LiAlH$_4$\n(c) C$_6$H$_5$NO$_2$, reduced with Sn/HCl followed by base\n(d) C$_6$H$_5$CH$_2$Cl, heated with large excess of ethanolic NH$_3$\nList-II\n(i) N-Methylethanamine\n(ii) Propan-1-amine\n(iii) Phenylmethanamine\n(iv) Benzenamine","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) LiAlH$_4$ converts –C≡N into –CH$_2$NH$_2$. CH$_3$CH$_2$CN gives CH$_3$CH$_2$CH$_2$NH$_2$, which is propan-1-amine → (ii).\n(b) LiAlH$_4$ converts the C=O of an amide into CH$_2$. CH$_3$CONHCH$_3$ gives CH$_3$CH$_2$NHCH$_3$, which is N-methylethanamine → (i).\n(c) Metal/acid reduces –NO$_2$ to –NH$_2$, giving aniline (benzenamine) → (iv).\n(d) Ammonolysis of benzyl chloride with excess NH$_3$ gives C$_6$H$_5$CH$_2$NH$_2$, which is phenylmethanamine → (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":9,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following aryl compounds cannot conveniently be made by direct halogenation of benzene and is instead prepared from benzenediazonium chloride?","image":"","options":["Chlorobenzene","Bromobenzene","Iodobenzene","Nitrobenzene"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that aryl fluorides and aryl iodides cannot be prepared by direct halogenation.\nIodobenzene is formed simply by treating a diazonium salt solution with KI.\nChlorobenzene and bromobenzene can be made by direct halogenation with a Lewis acid catalyst.\nNitrobenzene is not a halogen compound; it is made by direct nitration.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":10,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following isomers of $C_4H_{11}N$ cannot form intermolecular hydrogen bonds with its own molecules?","image":"","options":["Butan-1-amine","N-Ethylethanamine","N,N-Dimethylethanamine","2-Methylpropan-2-amine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"An amine needs an H atom on nitrogen to form an N–H···N hydrogen bond between its molecules.\nN,N-Dimethylethanamine is a tertiary amine with no H on nitrogen, so it shows no intermolecular association.\nThe other three are primary or secondary amines and all have N–H bonds.\n2-Methylpropan-2-amine has a tertiary carbon, but it is still a primary amine.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":11,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Although –NH$_2$ is an ortho/para directing group, direct nitration of aniline gives a significant amount of m-nitroaniline.\nReason (R): In the strongly acidic nitrating mixture, aniline is protonated to the anilinium ion, which is meta directing.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Direct nitration of aniline gives o- and p-nitroaniline along with a substantial amount of the meta product, plus tarry oxidation products.\nR is true. Conc. HNO$_3$/H$_2$SO$_4$ protonates –NH$_2$ to –NH$_3^+$. This group has no lone pair and withdraws electrons, so it directs the incoming group to the meta position.\nThe anilinium ion formed in the acid is exactly what produces the meta product, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":12,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following in increasing order of basic strength in aqueous solution:\n(I) p-Nitroaniline\n(II) Aniline\n(III) N-Methylaniline\n(IV) Phenylmethanamine\n(V) Ethanamine","image":"","options":["I < II < III < IV < V","II < I < III < V < IV","I < III < II < IV < V","I < II < IV < III < V"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(I) The –NO$_2$ group is electron withdrawing, so p-nitroaniline is weaker than aniline.\n(II) Aniline: $pK_b$ = 9.38.\n(III) N-methylaniline: $pK_b$ = 9.30. The +I effect of CH$_3$ makes it slightly stronger than aniline, but its lone pair is still conjugated with the ring.\n(IV) Benzylamine: $pK_b$ = 4.70. Its lone pair is not conjugated with the ring.\n(V) Ethanamine: $pK_b$ = 3.29. It is aliphatic and has a +I alkyl group.\nIncreasing basicity (decreasing $pK_b$): I < II < III < IV < V.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":13,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Which reagent sequence correctly converts 3-methylaniline into 3-nitrotoluene?","image":"","options":["$NaNO_2$/HCl at 273–278 K; then $HBF_4$; then aqueous $NaNO_2$ with Cu, heat","Conc. $HNO_3$ + conc. $H_2SO_4$ directly on 3-methylaniline","$NaNO_2$/HCl at 273–278 K; warm the solution to 283 K; then conc. $HNO_3$","$(CH_3CO)_2O$; then $HNO_3$/$H_2SO_4$; then hydrolysis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The –NH₂ group must be replaced by –NO₂ at the same carbon. Diazotisation gives 3-methylbenzenediazonium chloride.\nWith $HBF_4$ this forms the fluoroborate salt. Heating that salt with $NaNO_2$/Cu replaces the diazo group by –NO₂, giving 3-nitrotoluene.\n(2) Direct nitration gives tarry oxidation products and keeps –NH₂ in the product.\n(3) Warming to 283 K gives a phenol.\n(4) The product still contains –NH₂.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":14,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"8.2 g of ethanenitrile (CH$_3$CN) is completely reduced with LiAlH$_4$. Assuming 100% yield, what mass of amine is obtained? (Atomic masses: C = 12, H = 1, N = 14)","image":"","options":["9.0 g","8.2 g","4.5 g","6.2 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 8.2 g of CH$_3$CN, with M = 24 + 3 + 14 = 41 g/mol.\nReaction: CH$_3$CN + 4[H] → CH$_3$CH$_2$NH$_2$. This is a 1 : 1 mole ratio, and the amine keeps the same number of carbons as the nitrile (2).\nMoles of nitrile = 8.2 / 41 = 0.2 mol.\nMolar mass of ethanamine C$_2$H$_7$N = 24 + 7 + 14 = 45 g/mol.\nSubstituting: mass = 0.2 × 45 = 9.0 g.\nThe 6.2 g option wrongly assumes one carbon is lost, as in Hoffmann degradation.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":15,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (amine) with List-II (pKb in aqueous phase).\nList-I:\n(a) Methanamine\n(b) N,N-Dimethylmethanamine\n(c) Benzenamine\n(d) Phenylmethanamine\nList-II:\n(i) 9.38\n(ii) 4.70\n(iii) 3.38\n(iv) 4.22","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Methanamine, a 1° amine with no steric problem in solvation, has pKb 3.38, so (a)-(iii).\n(b) Trimethylamine is the poorly solvated 3° amine with pKb 4.22, so (b)-(iv).\n(c) Aniline is a very weak base because its lone pair is in conjugation with the ring. Its pKb is 9.38, so (c)-(i).\n(d) Benzylamine has its –NH$_2$ on a CH$_2$ group, not on the ring. Its pKb is 4.70, so (d)-(ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":16,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"9.3 g of aniline is completely diazotised by the reaction\n$C_6H_5NH_2 + NaNO_2 + 2HCl \\rightarrow C_6H_5N_2^+Cl^- + NaCl + 2H_2O$\nWhat minimum mass of sodium nitrite is required? (Molar masses: aniline = 93 g/mol, $NaNO_2$ = 69 g/mol)","image":"","options":["6.9 g","13.8 g","3.45 g","9.3 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: mass of aniline = 9.3 g, molar mass of aniline = 93 g/mol.\nMoles of aniline = 9.3/93 = 0.1 mol.\nFrom the equation, 1 mol aniline needs 1 mol $NaNO_2$. The factor 2 belongs to HCl, not to $NaNO_2$.\nMass of $NaNO_2$ = 0.1 × 69 = 6.9 g.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":17,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (reaction) with List-II (reagent used):\nList-I\n(a) Sandmeyer reaction\n(b) Gattermann reaction\n(c) Hoffmann bromamide degradation\n(d) Gabriel synthesis\nList-II\n(i) Br2 with aqueous NaOH on an amide\n(ii) Cu(I) salt with diazonium salt\n(iii) Ethanolic KOH, then alkyl halide, then alkaline hydrolysis\n(iv) Copper powder and halogen acid with diazonium salt","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Sandmeyer: Cl⁻, Br⁻ or CN⁻ replaces the diazo group in the presence of Cu(I), so (ii).\n(b) Gattermann: the diazonium salt is treated with a halogen acid and copper powder, so (iv).\n(c) Hoffmann bromamide: an amide reacts with Br2 in aqueous/ethanolic NaOH, so (i).\n(d) Gabriel: phthalimide with ethanolic KOH, then an alkyl halide, then alkaline hydrolysis, so (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":18,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about the basic strength of amines:\n(i) In the gas phase, $(C_2H_5)_3N$ is a stronger base than $(C_2H_5)_2NH$.\n(ii) In aqueous solution, $(CH_3)_3N$ is a weaker base than $CH_3NH_2$.\n(iii) Phenylmethanamine ($C_6H_5CH_2NH_2$) is a weaker base than aniline because the phenyl group withdraws electrons.\n(iv) In aqueous solution, N,N-dimethylaniline is a slightly stronger base than aniline.\n(v) Aniline is a weaker base than ammonia because the anilinium ion has more resonance structures than aniline.\nWhich of the statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(i), (ii) and (iv) only","(ii), (iii) and (v) only","(i), (iv) and (v) only","(i), (ii), (iii) and (iv) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. In the gas phase only the +I effect operates, giving the order 3° > 2° > 1°.\n(ii) Correct. In water, solvation and steric effects give pKb 4.22 for $(CH_3)_3N$ and 3.38 for $CH_3NH_2$.\n(iii) Incorrect. The $NH_2$ group in phenylmethanamine is not conjugated with the ring, so its pKb is 4.70, much lower than aniline's 9.38.\n(iv) Correct. The pKb values are 8.92 for N,N-dimethylaniline and 9.38 for aniline.\n(v) Incorrect. Aniline has five resonance structures, while the anilinium ion has only two.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":19,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Aniline is converted into benzenediazonium chloride with $NaNO_2$ and HCl. In which temperature range is this diazotisation carried out?","image":"","options":["253–263 K","273–278 K","298–308 K","353–373 K"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Arenediazonium salts are stable only for a short time in solution, and only at low temperature.\nNCERT therefore states that diazotisation is done at 273–278 K (0–5 °C).\nAt room temperature or above, the salt reacts with water. Near 283 K it already begins to hydrolyse to phenol, so 298–308 K and higher ranges are unsuitable.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":20,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following sequence:\n4-Methylaniline $\\xrightarrow{NaNO_2/HCl,\\ 273\\ K}$ A $\\xrightarrow{CuCN}$ B $\\xrightarrow{LiAlH_4}$ C $\\xrightarrow{NaNO_2/HCl}$ D + $N_2$\nWhat is D?","image":"","options":["(4-Methylphenyl)methanol","4-Methylbenzenediazonium chloride","4-Methylphenol","4-Methylbenzaldehyde"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is formed by diazotisation: 4-methylbenzenediazonium chloride.\nB is formed by the Sandmeyer reaction with CuCN: 4-methylbenzonitrile.\nC is formed when $LiAlH_4$ reduces the nitrile to a primary amine, $4\\text{-}CH_3C_6H_4CH_2NH_2$. The –NH₂ is on a CH₂ group, so C is a primary aliphatic-type amine.\nPrimary aliphatic amines react with nitrous acid to give an unstable diazonium salt, which releases N₂ and forms an alcohol. So D is (4-methylphenyl)methanol.\nOption (2) wrongly treats C as an arylamine. Option (3) is the product only when A is warmed.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":21,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (reagent used with amines) with List-II (purpose/result).\nList-I:\n(A) $CHCl_3$ + ethanolic KOH\n(B) $C_6H_5SO_2Cl$\n(C) $NaNO_2$ + HCl at 273–278 K with aniline\n(D) Acetic anhydride\nList-II:\n(I) Diazotisation\n(II) Protection of the –NH2 group as an amide\n(III) Test for primary amines\n(IV) Distinction and separation of 1°, 2° and 3° amines","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A)–(III): chloroform with alcoholic KOH is the carbylamine test, which is given only by primary amines.\n(B)–(IV): Hinsberg's reagent reacts differently with 1°, 2° and 3° amines. It is used both to tell them apart and to separate a mixture of them.\n(C)–(I): aniline with nitrous acid in the cold gives a diazonium salt; this is diazotisation.\n(D)–(II): acetylation converts –NH2 to –NHCOCH3, which protects the group and moderates its activating effect.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":22,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"The pKb of methanamine is 3.38 and that of benzenamine is 9.38 at the same temperature. The ratio $\\frac{K_b(\\text{methanamine})}{K_b(\\text{benzenamine})}$ is approximately:","image":"","options":["$10^{6}$","6","$10^{3}$","2.8"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: pKb(CH3NH2) = 3.38 and pKb(C6H5NH2) = 9.38.\nFormula: $K_b = 10^{-pK_b}$, so the ratio is $10^{(9.38-3.38)}$.\nSubstituting: the ratio is $10^{6}$.\nSo methanamine is about a million times stronger as a base than aniline. This shows how much conjugation lowers the basicity of an arylamine.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":23,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Aniline does not undergo Friedel–Crafts alkylation or acylation. The correct reason is:","image":"","options":["The –NH$_2$ group is a meta director, so the electrophile cannot attack at the o/p positions","Aniline is too weakly activated to react with the carbocation or acylium ion","Aniline forms a salt with the Lewis acid AlCl$_3$; nitrogen becomes positively charged and strongly deactivates the ring","AlCl$_3$ oxidises aniline completely to tarry products before substitution can occur"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: the lone pair on nitrogen (a Lewis base) bonds to AlCl$_3$ (a Lewis acid) and forms a salt.\nNitrogen then carries a positive charge and acts as a strong deactivating group, so the ring does not react further.\nOption (1) is wrong because free –NH$_2$ is an o/p director.\nOption (2) is wrong because aniline is strongly activated.\nTarry oxidation products are associated with direct nitration, not with AlCl$_3$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":24,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the basicity of aliphatic amines in aqueous solution are correct?\n(i) Among the methyl-substituted ammonium ions, $CH_3NH_3^+$ is the most stabilised by hydrogen bonding with water.\n(ii) In the ethyl series, $(C_2H_5)_3N$ is a stronger base than $C_2H_5NH_2$.\n(iii) In the methyl series, $(CH_3)_3N$ is a stronger base than $CH_3NH_2$.\n(iv) Every aliphatic amine listed in NCERT Table 9.3 is a stronger base than ammonia ($pK_b$ = 4.75).\n(v) Changing the alkyl group from –CH$_3$ to –C$_2$H$_5$ changes the basicity order, because larger groups cause steric hindrance to H-bonding.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. The 1° ion has three N–H bonds, so it is solvated the most.\n(ii) Correct. The aqueous order is $(C_2H_5)_2NH > (C_2H_5)_3N > C_2H_5NH_2$, with pKb 3.25 vs 3.29.\n(iii) Incorrect. The pKb of $(CH_3)_3N$ is 4.22, higher than 3.38 for $CH_3NH_2$, so it is weaker.\n(iv) Correct. All of these pKb values lie between 3.00 and 4.22, which is below 4.75.\n(v) Correct. This is the interplay of the inductive effect, solvation and steric hindrance.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":25,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) In the gas phase, basicity follows the order: tertiary amine > secondary amine > primary amine > NH$_3$.\n(ii) Among methyl-substituted amines in water, (CH$_3$)$_2$NH is the strongest base.\n(iii) The larger the substituted ammonium ion, the more extensively it is solvated by water, so the more stable it is.\n(iv) Aniline is less basic than ammonia partly because aniline has five resonating structures while the anilinium ion has only two.\n(v) p-Nitroaniline is a stronger base than aniline.\n(vi) N,N-Dimethylaniline is a stronger base than aniline.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: in the gas phase only the +I effect operates.\n(ii) Correct: the aqueous order is (CH$_3$)$_2$NH > CH$_3$NH$_2$ > (CH$_3$)$_3$N.\n(iii) Incorrect: a bigger ion is solvated less, and so it is less stabilised.\n(iv) Correct: aniline is stabilised by resonance more than its cation, so it accepts a proton less readily.\n(v) Incorrect: –NO$_2$ withdraws electrons and lowers basicity.\n(vi) Correct: the pKb is 8.92 for N,N-dimethylaniline against 9.38 for aniline.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":26,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In aqueous solution, triethylamine is a slightly stronger base than ethylamine.\nReason (R): A tertiary ammonium cation is solvated by water molecules to a greater extent than a primary ammonium cation.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The pKb of $(C_2H_5)_3N$ is 3.25, which is lower than 3.29 for $C_2H_5NH_2$, so triethylamine is slightly more basic.\nR is false. $R_3NH^+$ has only one N–H and is larger in size, so it is the least solvated cation, not the most.\nIn the ethyl series, the bulky groups and the +I effect outweigh the solvation advantage of the 1° cation.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":27,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Benzenediazonium chloride is treated separately with each reagent or condition below. In how many cases is nitrogen gas evolved?\nCuCN; KI; $H_3PO_2$ and water; warming the solution to 283 K; phenol (alkaline); aniline; $HBF_4$ followed by heating","image":"","options":["3","4","5","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Reactions that displace nitrogen:\n- CuCN gives benzonitrile.\n- KI gives iodobenzene.\n- $H_3PO_2$ gives benzene.\n- Warming to 283 K gives phenol.\n- $HBF_4$ then heat gives fluorobenzene.\nThat is 5 cases.\nCoupling with phenol (p-hydroxyazobenzene) and with aniline (p-aminoazobenzene) keeps the –N=N– group, so no $N_2$ is lost.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":28,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The amine obtained by the Hoffmann bromamide degradation contains one carbon atom less than the starting amide.\nReason (R): In this reaction, the alkyl or aryl group migrates from the carbonyl carbon of the amide to the nitrogen atom.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. For example, butanamide (4 C) gives propanamine (3 C).\nR is true. NCERT states that the alkyl or aryl group migrates from the carbonyl carbon to nitrogen.\nR explains A. Because the R group shifts onto nitrogen, the carbonyl carbon is no longer part of the amine and is lost. This is exactly why the amine has one carbon fewer.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":29,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Match each reagent used on benzenediazonium chloride (List-I) with the main organic product (List-II).\nList-I:\n(a) KI solution\n(b) $HBF_4$, then heat\n(c) CuCN\n(d) $H_3PO_2$ and water\nList-II:\n(i) Benzene\n(ii) Benzonitrile\n(iii) Iodobenzene\n(iv) Fluorobenzene","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-iv, c-ii, d-i","a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i","a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii","a-ii, b-iv, c-iii, d-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) KI replaces the diazonium group by I, giving iodobenzene: a-iii.\n(b) $HBF_4$ precipitates the fluoroborate salt, which on heating gives fluorobenzene: b-iv.\n(c) CuCN is a Sandmeyer reagent and gives benzonitrile: c-ii.\n(d) Hypophosphorous acid is a mild reducing agent that replaces the diazonium group by H, giving benzene: d-i.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":30,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following compounds gives a foul-smelling product when heated with chloroform and ethanolic potassium hydroxide?","image":"","options":["N-Methylaniline","Aniline","N,N-Dimethylaniline","Triethylamine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: the carbylamine (isocyanide) test is given only by primary amines, whether aliphatic or aromatic.\nAniline (C6H5NH2) is a primary arylamine. It forms phenyl isocyanide, which has a foul smell.\nN-Methylaniline is secondary. N,N-dimethylaniline and triethylamine are tertiary. Secondary and tertiary amines do not give this test.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":31,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Tertiary amines react with benzenesulphonyl chloride to form a sulphonamide that is insoluble in alkali.\nStatement II: The sulphonamide obtained from a primary amine and benzenesulphonyl chloride dissolves in alkali because its N–H hydrogen is made strongly acidic by the electron-withdrawing sulphonyl group.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Tertiary amines have no N–H and do not react with Hinsberg's reagent at all. The alkali-insoluble sulphonamide comes from secondary amines, such as N,N-diethylbenzenesulphonamide.\nStatement II is correct. The –SO$_2$– group makes the remaining N–H acidic, so the product from a primary amine dissolves in alkali.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":32,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Freshly distilled aniline is almost colourless, but a sample kept in a bottle for several weeks turns brown. According to NCERT, what causes this colour change?","image":"","options":["Atmospheric oxidation of aniline on storage","Absorption of moisture, which makes aniline form hydrogen bonds with water","Polymerisation of aniline caused by light into polyaniline fibres","Reaction of aniline with atmospheric nitrogen to form an azo dye"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that aniline and other arylamines are usually colourless.\nThey become coloured on storage because air oxidises them.\nHydrogen bonding with absorbed water does not produce colour, so option (2) is wrong.\nAzo dyes form only by coupling a diazonium salt with a phenol or an arylamine. Atmospheric $N_2$ does not do this, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":33,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the physical properties of amines is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Aniline is usually colourless when fresh but becomes coloured on storage due to atmospheric oxidation","Solubility of amines in water decreases as their molar mass increases","A tertiary amine boils at a higher temperature than an isomeric primary amine because it has more alkyl groups","Butan-1-ol has a higher boiling point than butan-1-amine because O–H…O hydrogen bonds are stronger than N–H…N hydrogen bonds"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: arylamines darken on storage because air oxidises them.\n(2) Correct: a larger hydrophobic alkyl part lowers solubility in water.\n(3) Incorrect: tertiary amines have no N–H, so they cannot form intermolecular hydrogen bonds. The order for isomers is primary > secondary > tertiary, e.g. 350.8 K vs 310.5 K for $C_4H_{11}N$ isomers.\n(4) Correct: oxygen is more electronegative, so alcohols form stronger hydrogen bonds (390.3 K vs 350.8 K).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":34,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Match each pair of compounds in List-I with the most suitable test in List-II that distinguishes them. Each test is used once.\nList-I\n(A) Methylamine and dimethylamine\n(B) Ethylamine and aniline\n(C) Diethylamine and triethylamine\n(D) Aniline and benzene\nList-II\n(i) NaNO2/HCl at 273–278 K, followed by coupling with phenol\n(ii) Benzenesulphonyl chloride\n(iii) CHCl3 and ethanolic KOH on heating\n(iv) Solubility in dilute HCl","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(C) Diethylamine forms a sulphonamide with benzenesulphonyl chloride; triethylamine does not react. Test (ii) is needed here.\n(B) Both are primary amines, so the carbylamine test fails. Only aniline gives a diazonium salt that couples with phenol to form a coloured azo dye. Test (i).\n(A) Only methylamine, a primary amine, gives an isocyanide with CHCl3/KOH. Test (iii).\n(D) Aniline, being basic, dissolves in dilute HCl as a salt; benzene does not. Test (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":35,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (amine) with List-II (pKb in aqueous phase):\nList-I\n(a) Methanamine\n(b) N,N-Dimethylmethanamine\n(c) N-Ethylethanamine\n(d) N,N-Diethylethanamine\nList-II\n(i) 3.00\n(ii) 3.25\n(iii) 3.38\n(iv) 4.22","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Methanamine, $CH_3NH_2$: pKb 3.38, so (iii).\n(b) Trimethylamine, $(CH_3)_3N$: pKb 4.22, the weakest of these because of poor solvation, so (iv).\n(c) Diethylamine, $(C_2H_5)_2NH$: pKb 3.00, the strongest, so (i).\n(d) Triethylamine, $(C_2H_5)_3N$: pKb 3.25, so (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":36,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"An amine X has the formula C3H9N. X reacts with benzenesulphonyl chloride to give a product that is insoluble in aqueous KOH. X does not liberate nitrogen gas with nitrous acid. X is:","image":"","options":["Propan-1-amine","Propan-2-amine","N-Methylethanamine","N,N-Dimethylmethanamine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"X reacts with Hinsberg's reagent, so X is not tertiary. This rules out N,N-dimethylmethanamine, which does not react at all.\nThe product is insoluble in alkali, so it has no N–H. That means X is a secondary amine.\nPrimary aliphatic amines (propan-1-amine, propan-2-amine) would evolve N2 with HNO2 and give alkali-soluble sulphonamides.\nThe only secondary amine with formula C3H9N is CH3NHC2H5, N-methylethanamine.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":37,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"1.18 g of propan-1-amine (molar mass = 59 g/mol) is treated with excess nitrous acid. The aliphatic diazonium salt formed decomposes and releases nitrogen quantitatively. What volume of N2 is obtained at STP? (Molar volume at STP = 22.4 L/mol)","image":"","options":["112 mL","224 mL","448 mL","896 mL"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: mass of amine = 1.18 g, M = 59 g/mol, molar volume = 22.4 L/mol.\nFormula: 1 mol of RNH2 gives 1 mol of N2, so moles of N2 = mass/M.\nSubstituting: moles = 1.18/59 = 0.02 mol, and volume = 0.02 × 22400 mL.\nSo the volume of N2 = 448 mL.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":38,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the sequence:\nBenzamide $\\xrightarrow{Br_2/NaOH}$ A $\\xrightarrow{NaNO_2/HCl,\\ 273\\text{ K}}$ B $\\xrightarrow{CuCN}$ C $\\xrightarrow{LiAlH_4}$ D\nWhich statement about D is correct?","image":"","options":["D is aniline, which is a weaker base than ammonia","D is phenylmethanamine, which is a stronger base than aniline","D is 2-phenylethanamine, which gives the carbylamine test","D is N-methylaniline, which forms an alkali-insoluble product with Hinsberg's reagent"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: Hoffmann degradation of benzamide removes one carbon, giving aniline (A).\nStep 2: diazotisation at 273–278 K gives benzenediazonium chloride (B).\nStep 3: Sandmeyer reaction with CuCN gives benzonitrile (C).\nStep 4: $LiAlH_4$ reduces the nitrile to $C_6H_5CH_2NH_2$ (D), phenylmethanamine.\nIn D the –$NH_2$ is not on the ring, so its lone pair is not delocalised. Its pKb is 4.70 against 9.38 for aniline. Option 3 adds one carbon too many.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":39,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Gabriel phthalimide synthesis gives primary amines free from secondary and tertiary amines.\nStatement II: Chlorobenzene reacts readily with potassium phthalimide, so aniline is conveniently prepared by Gabriel synthesis.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The nitrogen of phthalimide carries only one replaceable H, so only one alkyl group is attached. Hydrolysis therefore gives only the primary amine. This avoids the mixture of products that ammonolysis gives.\nStatement II is incorrect. Aryl halides do not undergo nucleophilic substitution with the phthalimide anion, so aromatic primary amines cannot be made by this method.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":40,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The solubility of aliphatic amines in water decreases as their molar mass increases.\nStatement II: A tertiary amine has a higher boiling point than its isomeric secondary amine because it carries more alkyl groups.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. A larger hydrophobic alkyl part lowers solubility, and higher amines are essentially insoluble in water.\nStatement II is false. Tertiary amines have no H on nitrogen and so show no intermolecular association.\nIsomeric amines therefore follow the boiling point order 1° > 2° > 3°.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":41,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following compounds will NOT give a foul-smelling product when heated with chloroform and ethanolic potassium hydroxide?","image":"","options":["Aniline","Ethanamine","N-Methylaniline","Phenylmethanamine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The carbylamine (isocyanide) test is given only by primary amines, whether aliphatic or aromatic.\nAniline, ethanamine and phenylmethanamine (benzylamine) are all primary amines, so each forms a foul-smelling isocyanide.\nN-Methylaniline, C6H5NHCH3, is a secondary amine and does not show this reaction.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":42,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Which sequence correctly converts benzyl chloride ($C_6H_5CH_2Cl$) into 2-phenylethanamine ($C_6H_5CH_2CH_2NH_2$)?","image":"","options":["Potassium phthalimide, then alkaline hydrolysis","KCN (ethanolic), then partial hydrolysis to the amide, then $Br_2$/NaOH","KCN (ethanolic), then reduction with $LiAlH_4$","Aqueous NH$_3$ in a sealed tube, then $Br_2$/NaOH"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Step 1: The target has one carbon more than the CH$_2$ chain of benzyl chloride, so a step-up is needed.\nStep 2: KCN gives C$_6$H$_5$CH$_2$CN. LiAlH$_4$ then reduces the nitrile to C$_6$H$_5$CH$_2$CH$_2$NH$_2$. This option is correct.\nOption (1): Gabriel synthesis gives benzylamine, with no extra carbon.\nOption (2): C$_6$H$_5$CH$_2$CONH$_2$ under Hoffmann conditions loses a carbon and gives benzylamine.\nOption (4): ammonolysis gives benzylamine, and an amine does not undergo the Hoffmann reaction.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":43,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Match the common names in List-I with the IUPAC names in List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Allylamine\n(B) Hexamethylenediamine\n(C) o-Toluidine\n(D) Ethylmethylamine\nList-II:\n(I) N-Methylethanamine\n(II) 2-Methylaniline\n(III) Prop-2-en-1-amine\n(IV) Hexane-1,6-diamine","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Allylamine is CH2=CH–CH2–NH2, named prop-2-en-1-amine, so A matches III.\n(B) Hexamethylenediamine is H2N–(CH2)6–NH2, named hexane-1,6-diamine. The 'e' of hexane is retained, so B matches IV.\n(C) o-Toluidine has CH3 ortho to NH2 on the ring, so it is 2-methylaniline (C–II).\n(D) Ethylmethylamine is CH3NHC2H5. The longer chain is the parent and methyl takes the N-locant, giving N-methylethanamine (D–I).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":44,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following compounds of nearly the same molar mass (72 to 74 g/mol):\n(P) n-butylamine, $n\\text{-}C_4H_9NH_2$\n(Q) diethylamine, $(C_2H_5)_2NH$\n(R) N,N-dimethylethanamine, $C_2H_5N(CH_3)_2$\n(S) 2-methylbutane, $C_2H_5CH(CH_3)_2$\n(T) butan-1-ol, $n\\text{-}C_4H_9OH$\nWhich is the correct decreasing order of boiling points?","image":"","options":["T > P > Q > R > S","P > T > Q > R > S","T > Q > P > R > S","T > P > Q > S > R"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) The alcohol T boils highest (390.3 K). O (electronegativity 3.5) is more electronegative than N (3.0), so O–H...O hydrogen bonds are stronger than N–H...N bonds.\n(ii) P, a 1° amine with two N–H hydrogens, has the most association among the amines (350.8 K).\n(iii) Q, a 2° amine with one N–H, comes next (329.3 K).\n(iv) R, a 3° amine with no N–H, has no intermolecular H-bonding (310.5 K). It still boils above the alkane S (300.8 K) because it is polar.\nSo the order is T > P > Q > R > S.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":45,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In ammonolysis, the reactivity of alkyl halides follows the order RI > RBr > RCl.\nStatement II: The free amine can be obtained from the substituted ammonium salt formed in ammonolysis by treating the salt with a strong base.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The C–I bond is the weakest, so iodide leaves most easily. Hence the order is RI > RBr > RCl.\nStatement II is correct. Ammonolysis gives the amine as an ammonium halide salt. A strong base such as NaOH removes the proton and releases the free amine.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":46,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In aqueous solution, triethylamine is a stronger base than ethylamine.\nStatement II: In aqueous solution, trimethylamine is a stronger base than methylamine.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In water, basicity depends on the inductive effect, solvation and steric hindrance together.\nStatement I is correct: the ethyl order is $(C_2H_5)_2NH > (C_2H_5)_3N > C_2H_5NH_2 > NH_3$. The pKb values are 3.25 for triethylamine and 3.29 for ethylamine.\nStatement II is incorrect: the methyl order is $(CH_3)_2NH > CH_3NH_2 > (CH_3)_3N > NH_3$. Trimethylamine has pKb 4.22 against 3.38 for methylamine, so it is the weaker base.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":47,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Ethanamine is to be prepared from phthalimide by Gabriel synthesis. The correct sequence of reagents is:","image":"","options":["Ethanolic KOH; then $C_2H_5Br$ with heating; then alkaline hydrolysis","$C_2H_5Br$; then ethanolic KOH; then $Br_2$/NaOH","Ethanolic KOH; then $LiAlH_4$; then $C_2H_5Br$","$Br_2$/NaOH; then $C_2H_5Br$; then ethanolic KOH"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: Ethanolic KOH converts phthalimide into potassium phthalimide.\nStep 2: Heating the salt with ethyl bromide gives N-ethylphthalimide by N-alkylation.\nStep 3: Alkaline hydrolysis releases the primary amine $C_2H_5NH_2$.\nOption (2) fails because the salt must be formed before alkylation. $Br_2$/NaOH is the reagent for Hoffmann degradation, not Gabriel synthesis.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":48,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"An amide on treatment with bromine in aqueous sodium hydroxide gives butan-1-amine ($CH_3CH_2CH_2CH_2NH_2$). The amide used is:","image":"","options":["Butanamide","Pentanamide","Propanamide","2-Methylpropanamide"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Hoffmann bromamide degradation gives an amine with one carbon less than the starting amide, because the carbonyl carbon is lost.\nButan-1-amine has 4 carbons, so the amide must have 5 carbons in a straight chain. That amide is pentanamide, $CH_3(CH_2)_3CONH_2$.\nButanamide (4 C) would give propan-1-amine.\n2-Methylpropanamide has a branched chain and would give propan-2-amine.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":49,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (amine) with List-II (pKb in aqueous phase):\nList-I\n(a) Benzenamine\n(b) Phenylmethanamine\n(c) N-Methylaniline\n(d) N,N-Dimethylaniline\nList-II\n(i) 8.92\n(ii) 9.38\n(iii) 4.70\n(iv) 9.30","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Benzylamine is not conjugated with the ring, so it has the lowest pKb, 4.70: (b)-(iii).\nAniline, with no alkyl group on nitrogen, is the weakest of the arylamines here, so it has pKb 9.38: (a)-(ii).\nEach methyl group on nitrogen releases electrons and raises basicity slightly. N-methylaniline therefore has pKb 9.30: (c)-(iv).\nN,N-dimethylaniline has the lowest pKb of the three arylamines, 8.92: (d)-(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":50,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"A freshly distilled sample of aniline is colourless, but it slowly turns coloured when stored in a bottle. The main reason is:","image":"","options":["absorption of moisture followed by hydrolysis","polymerisation caused by sunlight","oxidation by atmospheric air","reaction with atmospheric carbon dioxide forming a salt"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that aniline and other arylamines are usually colourless.\nThey become coloured on storage because of atmospheric oxidation.\nAniline does not hydrolyse with moisture, so option (1) does not apply.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":51,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Aniline does not undergo Friedel-Crafts alkylation or acylation.\nReason (R): The –NH2 group is ortho and para directing in electrophilic substitution.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. Aniline, a Lewis base, forms a salt with the catalyst AlCl3, a Lewis acid. The nitrogen then carries a positive charge and strongly deactivates the ring.\nR is also true. The –NH2 group raises electron density at the ortho and para positions.\nHowever, ortho/para direction does not explain why the Friedel-Crafts reaction fails. The real cause is salt formation with AlCl3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":52,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Aniline is shaken with bromine water at room temperature. What is observed?","image":"","options":["A colourless solution of p-bromoaniline forms","A mixture of o- and p-bromoaniline forms in about equal amounts","A yellow precipitate of m-bromoaniline forms","A white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromoaniline forms"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The –NH$_2$ group pushes its lone pair into the ring by resonance. This makes the ortho and para positions very electron-rich.\nAniline is so strongly activated that bromine water substitutes all three o/p positions at room temperature, without any catalyst.\nThe product, 2,4,6-tribromoaniline, separates as a white precipitate.\nMonobromination (the tempting option) needs the –NH$_2$ group to be protected first. Bromination at the meta position does not occur.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":53,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Benzyl chloride is heated with a large excess of ethanolic ammonia, and the free amine X is released from its salt by treatment with a strong base. X is then allowed to react with exactly two moles of CH$_3$Cl, and the free amine Y is liberated by treatment with base. What is Y?","image":"","options":["N-Methylphenylmethanamine","N,N-Dimethylaniline","Benzyltrimethylammonium chloride","N,N-Dimethylphenylmethanamine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Step 1: Ammonolysis with excess NH$_3$ gives the salt C$_6$H$_5$CH$_2$NH$_3^+$Cl$^-$. A strong base releases X = C$_6$H$_5$CH$_2$NH$_2$ (phenylmethanamine).\nStep 2: Each mole of CH$_3$Cl replaces one N–H hydrogen. Two moles give C$_6$H$_5$CH$_2$N(CH$_3$)$_2$, released as the free amine by base.\nOption (1) needs only one mole of CH$_3$Cl. Option (3) would need a third mole.\nOption (2) has nitrogen attached directly to the ring, which cannot form from benzyl chloride.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":54,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly describes the lower aliphatic amines, such as methanamine and ethanamine, at room temperature?","image":"","options":["Gases with a fishy odour","Liquids with a fruity odour","Odourless colourless gases","Crystalline solids with a pungent odour"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the lower aliphatic amines are gases with a fishy odour.\nPrimary amines with three or more carbon atoms are liquids, and still higher ones are solids.\nA fruity odour is typical of esters, not amines.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":55,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Each of the following groups is placed at the para position of aniline:\n–NO$_2$, –OCH$_3$, –SO$_3$H, –CH$_3$, –COOH, –Cl\nHow many of them decrease the basic strength compared with aniline?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Electron-withdrawing groups lower the electron density on nitrogen and so decrease basicity.\n–NO2, –SO3H, –COOH and –Cl (a halogen, –X) are all electron-withdrawing, so they decrease basicity.\n–OCH3 and –CH3 are electron-releasing, so they increase basicity.\nThe count of groups that decrease basicity is 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":56,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The C–N–C bond angle in trimethylamine is about 108°.\nReason (R): The nitrogen in trimethylamine is $sp^3$ hybridised, and one of its hybrid orbitals holds an unshared electron pair that compresses the bond angles.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT gives the C–N–C angle in trimethylamine as 108°, which gives the molecule a pyramidal shape.\nR is true. Nitrogen uses three $sp^3$ orbitals for bonding, and the fourth orbital holds a lone pair.\nLone pair–bond pair repulsion is greater than bond pair–bond pair repulsion, so the angle shrinks below 109.5°.\nR therefore correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":57,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about electrophilic substitution in aniline is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Aniline reacts with bromine water at room temperature to give a white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromoaniline.","Direct nitration of aniline gives a significant amount of m-nitroaniline because the anilinium ion formed in strongly acidic medium is meta directing.","Acetylation of the –NH$_2$ group increases its activating effect, because the nitrogen lone pair is pushed into the ring by resonance with the carbonyl oxygen.","Aniline does not undergo Friedel–Crafts reaction because it forms a salt with AlCl$_3$, giving nitrogen a positive charge."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the –NH$_2$ group is strongly activating, so bromine water gives tribromination at once.\n(2) Correct: protonation to C$_6$H$_5$NH$_3^+$ makes the substituent meta directing.\n(3) Incorrect: in acetanilide the nitrogen lone pair is delocalised towards the carbonyl oxygen. It is therefore less available to the ring, so –NHCOCH$_3$ is less activating than –NH$_2$. This is why acetylation is used to control substitution.\n(4) Correct: salt formation with the Lewis acid AlCl$_3$ turns the group into a strong deactivator.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":58,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Aniline reacts with concentrated $H_2SO_4$ to form anilinium hydrogensulphate. When this salt is heated with sulphuric acid at 453–473 K, the major product is:","image":"","options":["o-Aminobenzenesulphonic acid","m-Aminobenzenesulphonic acid","Benzenesulphonic acid","p-Aminobenzenesulphonic acid (sulphanilic acid)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Step 1: Aniline + conc. H2SO4 gives anilinium hydrogensulphate.\nStep 2: Heating this salt with H2SO4 at 453–473 K gives p-aminobenzenesulphonic acid as the major product. It is commonly called sulphanilic acid.\nThe –NH2 group is retained, so benzenesulphonic acid is not formed.\nThe para isomer, not the meta isomer, is the major product.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":59,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The C–N–C bond angle in trimethylamine is about $108^\\circ$.\nReason (R): The nitrogen of trimethylamine is $sp^3$ hybridised, and its unshared electron pair compresses the bond angles below the tetrahedral value.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT gives the C–N–C angle in trimethylamine as $108^\\circ$, giving a pyramidal shape.\nR is true: nitrogen uses four $sp^3$ orbitals. Three form bonds and the fourth holds a lone pair.\nThe lone pair repels the bond pairs more strongly than they repel each other. This squeezes the angle below $109.5^\\circ$, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":60,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"11.8 g of ethanamide ($CH_3CONH_2$) is completely converted by the Hoffmann bromamide reaction. Assuming 100% yield, what mass of amine is obtained? (C = 12, H = 1, N = 14, O = 16)","image":"","options":["6.2 g","9.0 g","11.8 g","3.1 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: mass of ethanamide = 11.8 g. Molar mass of CH$_3$CONH$_2$ = 59 g/mol.\nFormula: 1 mol of amide gives 1 mol of amine with one carbon fewer, so the product is CH$_3$NH$_2$ (molar mass 31 g/mol).\nSubstituting: moles of amide = 11.8/59 = 0.2 mol. Mass of amine = 0.2 × 31 = 6.2 g.\n9.0 g would be the answer only if ethanamine (45 g/mol) formed, but it does not.\nSo the mass of methanamine = 6.2 g.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":61,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The –NHCOCH3 group of acetanilide activates the benzene ring less than the –NH2 group of aniline.\nStatement II: In acetanilide, the lone pair on nitrogen is delocalised towards the carbonyl oxygen by resonance, so it is less available for donation to the benzene ring.","image":"","options":["Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Acetylation tones down the activation, which is why monosubstitution becomes possible.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT shows the resonance in which the nitrogen lone pair interacts with the oxygen of the C=O group.\nWith the lone pair shared with the carbonyl, less electron density is pushed into the ring. Statement II therefore explains Statement I.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":62,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Benzamide is heated with bromine in aqueous sodium hydroxide. The organic product formed is:","image":"","options":["Phenylmethanamine (benzylamine)","Benzenamine (aniline)","N-Methylaniline","Benzonitrile"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: This is the Hoffmann bromamide degradation. The amine formed has one carbon atom fewer than the amide.\nStep 2: Benzamide, C$_6$H$_5$CONH$_2$, has 7 carbons, so the amine has 6 carbons.\nStep 3: The phenyl group migrates to nitrogen, giving C$_6$H$_5$NH$_2$, which is aniline.\nBenzylamine is tempting, but it has 7 carbons. It forms when benzamide is reduced with LiAlH$_4$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":63,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Primary aliphatic amines react with nitrous acid to give alcohols, with quantitative evolution of N2; this is used to estimate amino acids","Aniline reacts with nitrous acid at 273–278 K to form a diazonium salt","Ethanamine reacts with acetic acid at room temperature to give N-ethylethanamide","N,N-Dimethylmethanamine cannot be benzoylated because its nitrogen carries no hydrogen atom"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. Alkyldiazonium salts are unstable and decompose to give N2 quantitatively and alcohols.\n(2) Correct. Arenediazonium salts form at 273–278 K and are stable for a short time at that temperature.\n(3) Incorrect. At room temperature, amines and carboxylic acids only form salts. Amides are made using acid chlorides, anhydrides or esters.\n(4) Correct. Acylation replaces a hydrogen on nitrogen, so a tertiary amine cannot be acylated.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":64,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"The pKb of methanamine is 3.38 and that of benzenamine (aniline) is 9.38. By what factor is $K_b$ of methanamine greater than $K_b$ of benzenamine?","image":"","options":["$10^{6}$","About 2.8","6","$10^{-6}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: pKb (methanamine) = 3.38 and pKb (benzenamine) = 9.38.\nFormula: $K_b = 10^{-pK_b}$, so the ratio of the two $K_b$ values is $10^{(pK_{b2} - pK_{b1})}$.\nSubstituting: ratio = $10^{(9.38 - 3.38)} = 10^{6}$.\nSo methanamine is $10^6$ times stronger as a base than aniline.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":65,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Sandmeyer reaction\n(b) Gattermann reaction\n(c) Carbylamine reaction\n(d) Hinsberg test\nList-II\n(i) Diazonium salt + HCl in presence of copper powder\n(ii) Primary amine + $CHCl_3$ + ethanolic KOH\n(iii) Diazonium salt + $Cu_2Cl_2$/HCl\n(iv) Amine + benzenesulphonyl chloride","image":"","options":["(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Sandmeyer reaction uses a Cu(I) salt such as $Cu_2Cl_2$/HCl, so it matches (iii).\n(b) Gattermann reaction uses the halogen acid with copper powder, so it matches (i).\n(c) Carbylamine reaction is heating a primary amine with $CHCl_3$ and ethanolic KOH, so it matches (ii).\n(d) Hinsberg's reagent is $C_6H_5SO_2Cl$, so it matches (iv).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":66,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Which amide gives propan-1-amine on treatment with $Br_2$ and aqueous NaOH?","image":"","options":["Propanamide","Ethanamide","Butanamide","Pentanamide"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Step 1: In the Hoffmann bromamide reaction, the amine has one carbon fewer than the amide.\nStep 2: Propan-1-amine has 3 carbons, so the amide must have 4 carbons.\nStep 3: The straight-chain 4-carbon amide is butanamide, CH$_3$CH$_2$CH$_2$CONH$_2$. Its propyl group migrates to N to give CH$_3$CH$_2$CH$_2$NH$_2$.\nPropanamide would give ethanamine instead.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":67,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Benzenediazonium chloride is treated with phenol in a mildly alkaline medium. Which option correctly describes the product?","image":"","options":["p-Hydroxyazobenzene, with the phenol ring coupled at its para position","m-Hydroxyazobenzene, with the phenol ring coupled at its meta position","Phenol and chlorobenzene, with nitrogen gas evolved","Diphenyl ether, with nitrogen gas evolved"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: the phenol molecule couples with benzenediazonium chloride at its para position. The product is p-hydroxyazobenzene.\nThe –N=N– group is retained, so no nitrogen gas is lost. This rules out options (3) and (4).\nThe –OH group directs to ortho and para positions, never meta, so option (2) is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":68,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Consider all the structurally isomeric amines with molecular formula $C_4H_{11}N$. How many of them can show intermolecular hydrogen bonding among their own molecules in the pure liquid state?","image":"","options":["4","5","8","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Primary isomers (4): butan-1-amine, butan-2-amine, 2-methylpropan-1-amine and 2-methylpropan-2-amine.\nSecondary isomers (3): N-methylpropan-1-amine, N-methylpropan-2-amine and N-ethylethanamine.\nTertiary isomer (1): N,N-dimethylethanamine.\nOnly amines with an N–H bond can hydrogen bond with themselves, so 4 + 3 = 7 isomers.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":69,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"The correct decreasing order of basic strength of the methyl-substituted amines and ammonia in aqueous solution is:","image":"","options":["$(CH_3)_3N > (CH_3)_2NH > CH_3NH_2 > NH_3$","$(CH_3)_2NH > (CH_3)_3N > CH_3NH_2 > NH_3$","$CH_3NH_2 > (CH_3)_2NH > (CH_3)_3N > NH_3$","$(CH_3)_2NH > CH_3NH_2 > (CH_3)_3N > NH_3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The aqueous pKb values are: $(CH_3)_2NH$ 3.27, $CH_3NH_2$ 3.38, $(CH_3)_3N$ 4.22 and $NH_3$ 4.75.\nSince lower pKb means a stronger base, the order is 2° > 1° > 3° > $NH_3$.\nTrimethylamine falls below methylamine because its large cation is poorly solvated in water.\nOption (2), with 2° > 3° > 1°, is the ethyl-series pattern, not the methyl series.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":70,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, how many resonating structures can be written for aniline and for the anilinium ion, respectively?","image":"","options":["2 and 5","5 and 2","5 and 5","3 and 2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Aniline has five resonating structures: two Kekulé forms plus three forms where the nitrogen lone pair is delocalised to the ortho and para positions.\nThe anilinium ion has no lone pair on nitrogen, so only its two Kekulé structures are possible.\nMore resonance means greater stability, so aniline is stabilised more than its cation.\nThis makes protonation less favourable, which is why aniline is a weak base.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":71,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Gabriel phthalimide synthesis is preferred over ammonolysis of alkyl halides for preparing primary amines.\nReason (R): Aryl halides do not undergo nucleophilic substitution with the anion formed by phthalimide.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true: ammonolysis gives a mixture of primary, secondary and tertiary amines plus the quaternary salt. Gabriel synthesis gives only the primary amine.\nR is true: aryl halides do not react with the phthalimide anion, so aromatic primary amines cannot be made this way.\nR describes a limitation of the method. It does not explain why the method is preferred, which is that it gives a pure primary amine.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":72,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Match the compounds in List-I (each with molar mass close to 73 g/mol) with their boiling points in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) $n$-$C_4H_9OH$\n(B) $(C_2H_5)_2NH$\n(C) $C_2H_5N(CH_3)_2$\n(D) $n$-$C_4H_9NH_2$\nList-II\n(i) 310.5 K\n(ii) 329.3 K\n(iii) 350.8 K\n(iv) 390.3 K","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The alcohol forms the strongest hydrogen bonds, so A-(iv), 390.3 K.\nThe primary amine has two N–H hydrogens and the greatest association, so D-(iii), 350.8 K.\nThe secondary amine has one N–H hydrogen, so B-(ii), 329.3 K.\nThe tertiary amine cannot hydrogen bond with itself and boils lowest among the amines, so C-(i), 310.5 K.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":73,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"In trimethylamine, the C–N–C bond angle is about 108°, which is less than the tetrahedral angle of 109.5°. This is mainly because:","image":"","options":["Nitrogen is sp2 hybridised","The unshared electron pair on nitrogen occupies one sp3 orbital","The three methyl groups attract one another","Nitrogen forms a double bond with one carbon atom"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Nitrogen in amines is sp3 hybridised. Three of its orbitals form bonds and the fourth holds a lone pair.\nThe lone pair repels the bond pairs more strongly than they repel each other. This squeezes the C–N–C angle to about 108°, and the molecule is pyramidal.\nOption (1) is wrong because sp2 nitrogen would be planar with angles near 120°. There is also no C=N double bond in trimethylamine.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":74,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"What is the correct name of the compound $C_6H_5N_2^+HSO_4^-$?","image":"","options":["Phenyldiazo hydrogensulphate","Benzenediazonium hydrogensulphate","Anilinium hydrogensulphate","Benzeneazo sulphate"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Diazonium salts are named by adding 'diazonium' to the name of the parent hydrocarbon (benzene). The name of the anion follows.\nThe anion here is $HSO_4^-$, which is hydrogensulphate, so the name is benzenediazonium hydrogensulphate.\nAnilinium hydrogensulphate is $C_6H_5NH_3^+HSO_4^-$. That salt is formed by protonating aniline and has no $-N_2^+$ group.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":75,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"An amide P is heated with $Br_2$ and aqueous NaOH to give amine Q ($C_3H_9N$). Q reacts with nitrous acid, liberating nitrogen gas and forming a secondary alcohol. Identify P.","image":"","options":["Butanamide","Propanamide","2-Methylpropanamide","N-Methylpropanamide"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Step 1: Q liberates N$_2$ with HNO$_2$, so it is a primary aliphatic amine. The alcohol formed carries the OH where the NH$_2$ was.\nStep 2: The alcohol is secondary, so NH$_2$ is on a secondary carbon. Q is therefore propan-2-amine, (CH$_3$)$_2$CHNH$_2$.\nStep 3: The Hoffmann reaction removes one carbon, and the isopropyl group migrates to N. So P = (CH$_3$)$_2$CHCONH$_2$, which is 2-methylpropanamide.\nButanamide would give propan-1-amine, which gives a primary alcohol.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":76,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Using aqueous-phase pKb values, arrange the following in increasing order of basic strength:\n$C_6H_5NH_2$, $NH_3$, $C_6H_5CH_2NH_2$, $(CH_3)_3N$, $(CH_3)_2NH$","image":"","options":["$C_6H_5NH_2 < NH_3 < C_6H_5CH_2NH_2 < (CH_3)_3N < (CH_3)_2NH$","$C_6H_5NH_2 < C_6H_5CH_2NH_2 < NH_3 < (CH_3)_2NH < (CH_3)_3N$","$NH_3 < C_6H_5NH_2 < C_6H_5CH_2NH_2 < (CH_3)_3N < (CH_3)_2NH$","$C_6H_5NH_2 < NH_3 < (CH_3)_3N < C_6H_5CH_2NH_2 < (CH_3)_2NH$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The pKb values are: aniline 9.38, NH$_3$ 4.75, benzylamine 4.70, $(CH_3)_3N$ 4.22 and $(CH_3)_2NH$ 3.27.\nIncreasing basic strength means decreasing pKb.\nBenzylamine (4.70) is only slightly stronger than NH$_3$ (4.75), because its lone pair is not conjugated with the ring.\nIn water, $(CH_3)_3N$ is weaker than $(CH_3)_2NH$ due to poor solvation, which rules out option (2).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":77,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"In the Gabriel phthalimide synthesis, which reagent first converts phthalimide into its potassium salt?","image":"","options":["Ethanolic potassium hydroxide","Bromine in aqueous sodium hydroxide","Lithium aluminium hydride","Sodium nitrite and hydrochloric acid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: Phthalimide is treated with ethanolic KOH, which forms potassium phthalimide.\nStep 2: Potassium phthalimide is then heated with an alkyl halide to give the N-alkylphthalimide.\nStep 3: Alkaline hydrolysis of the N-alkylphthalimide releases the primary amine.\nBr$_2$/NaOH is the Hoffmann reagent, not a Gabriel reagent. LiAlH$_4$ reduces amides and nitriles, and NaNO$_2$/HCl is used for diazotisation.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":78,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Aniline does not undergo Friedel–Crafts alkylation or acylation.\nReason (R): The –$NH_2$ group is an electron-withdrawing group that deactivates the benzene ring.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: aniline forms a salt with the Lewis acid $AlCl_3$. Nitrogen then carries a positive charge and strongly deactivates the ring.\nR is false: free –$NH_2$ is a powerful activating, ortho/para-directing group, because its lone pair is delocalised into the ring.\nThe deactivation comes only from salt formation, not from –$NH_2$ itself.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":79,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about acylation of amines is correct?","image":"","options":["Tertiary amines are readily acylated by acid chlorides to give amides","Acylation is an electrophilic substitution reaction occurring at the nitrogen atom","Amines react with carboxylic acids at room temperature to give amides directly","Pyridine is added as a base stronger than the amine to remove HCl and shift the equilibrium forward"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: acylation replaces an H on nitrogen. Tertiary amines have no N–H, so they are not acylated.\n(2) Incorrect: NCERT describes acylation as a nucleophilic substitution, with the amine acting as the nucleophile.\n(3) Incorrect: at room temperature, carboxylic acids form salts with amines, not amides.\n(4) Correct: pyridine is a base stronger than the amine. It removes the HCl formed and pushes the equilibrium to the right.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":80,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Methanamine reacts with benzoyl chloride ($C_6H_5COCl$). What is the organic product, and what is this reaction called?","image":"","options":["N-Methylbenzamide; benzoylation","Benzamide; Hoffmann degradation","Methyl benzoate; esterification","N-Methylbenzenesulphonamide; Hinsberg reaction"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Reaction: $CH_3NH_2 + C_6H_5COCl \\rightarrow CH_3NHCOC_6H_5 + HCl$.\nThe benzoyl group replaces one H on nitrogen. The product is N-methylbenzamide.\nAcylation using benzoyl chloride is specifically called benzoylation.\nOption (4) is tempting, but a sulphonamide forms only with benzenesulphonyl chloride ($C_6H_5SO_2Cl$), not with benzoyl chloride.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":81,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Aniline cannot be prepared by Gabriel phthalimide synthesis.\nStatement II: Hoffmann bromamide degradation of benzamide gives aniline.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: aryl halides do not undergo nucleophilic substitution with the phthalimide anion. So aromatic primary amines cannot be made by Gabriel synthesis.\nStatement II is correct: Hoffmann degradation removes one carbon. Benzamide (7 C) therefore gives aniline (6 C), as in NCERT Example 9.3.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":82,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Iodobenzene is formed when a solution of benzenediazonium chloride is treated with potassium iodide.\nStatement II: The Sandmeyer reaction gives a better yield of halobenzenes than the Gattermann reaction.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Iodine is hard to introduce into benzene directly, but KI displaces $N_2$ from the diazonium salt and gives iodobenzene. No copper catalyst is needed.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT notes that the Sandmeyer reaction (Cu(I) salts) gives a better yield than the Gattermann reaction (Cu powder with HX).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":83,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When benzenediazonium chloride reacts with aniline to form p-aminoazobenzene, the diazo nitrogen atoms are retained in the product.\nStatement II: When benzenediazonium chloride is treated with CuCN to form benzonitrile, the nitrogen escapes from the reaction mixture as a gas.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Coupling is a reaction in which the diazo group is retained. The two rings are joined by –N=N– in p-aminoazobenzene.\nStatement II is correct. The Sandmeyer reaction with CuCN is a displacement reaction: CN replaces the diazonium group and N₂ gas escapes.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":84,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"In trimethylamine, the C–N–C bond angle is about 108°, which is less than the tetrahedral angle of 109.5°. Which of the following best explains this?","image":"","options":["Nitrogen is sp2 hybridised, so the molecule is planar","The unshared electron pair on sp3 nitrogen repels the bonding pairs more strongly, compressing the bond angle","The three methyl groups attract one another and pull the bonds closer","Nitrogen uses pure p orbitals for bonding, giving 90° angles that open slightly"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In amines, as in ammonia, nitrogen is trivalent and sp3 hybridised, so the shape is pyramidal.\nThree sp3 orbitals form bonds. The fourth orbital holds an unshared electron pair.\nThis lone pair repels the bond pairs more strongly than they repel each other, so the C–N–C angle shrinks to 108° in trimethylamine.\nOption (1) fails because sp2 nitrogen would give a planar shape with 120° angles.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":85,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Benzamide is treated with $Br_2$/NaOH to give A. A reacts with $NaNO_2$/HCl at 273–278 K to give B. B is treated with CuCN to give C, and C is reduced with $LiAlH_4$ to give D. Which statement about D is correct?","image":"","options":["D is aniline, and it gives the carbylamine test","D is phenylmethanamine, and it is a stronger base than aniline","D is phenylmethanamine, and it cannot be prepared by Gabriel phthalimide synthesis","D is N-methylaniline, and it does not give the carbylamine test"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Hoffmann degradation of benzamide (7 C) gives A = aniline (6 C).\nDiazotisation gives B = benzenediazonium chloride.\nSandmeyer reaction with CuCN gives C = benzonitrile, which adds one carbon.\n$LiAlH_4$ reduces the nitrile to D = $C_6H_5CH_2NH_2$, phenylmethanamine.\nIn D the $-NH_2$ is not attached to the ring, so its lone pair is not delocalised. Its pKb is 4.70, compared with 9.38 for aniline, so D is the stronger base.\nOption (3) is wrong because benzyl halides do react in Gabriel synthesis.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":86,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the synthetic uses of arenediazonium salts is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Benzonitrile is easily obtained from benzenediazonium chloride, though CN cannot be introduced by nucleophilic substitution of Cl in chlorobenzene","Aryl fluorides are obtained by heating the precipitated arenediazonium fluoroborate","Phenol is formed when the temperature of a benzenediazonium chloride solution is allowed to rise to about 283 K","In coupling with phenol, the diazonium ion acts as a nucleophile that attacks the phenol ring"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT notes that cyanobenzene is easily obtained from the diazonium salt but not from chlorobenzene.\n(2) Correct: $ArN_2^+BF_4^-$ gives ArF on heating.\n(3) Correct: the salt is hydrolysed to phenol at about 283 K.\n(4) Incorrect: coupling is an electrophilic substitution. The diazonium cation is the electrophile, and the electron-rich phenol ring is attacked by it.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":87,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following primary amines is expected to be the most soluble in water?","image":"","options":["Hexan-1-amine","Methanamine","Butan-1-amine","Octan-1-amine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Lower aliphatic amines dissolve in water because they form hydrogen bonds with water molecules.\nSolubility falls as molar mass rises, because the hydrophobic alkyl part becomes larger.\nMethanamine has the smallest alkyl group, so it is the most soluble.\nOctan-1-amine has the largest alkyl group and is essentially insoluble.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":88,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Reduction of acetamide (CH$_3$CONH$_2$) with LiAlH$_4$ gives ethanamine.\nStatement II: Reduction of an amide with LiAlH$_4$ gives an amine containing one carbon atom less than the amide.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: LiAlH$_4$ reduces the C=O group of the amide to CH$_2$. So CH$_3$CONH$_2$ → CH$_3$CH$_2$NH$_2$, which is ethanamine.\nStatement II is incorrect: LiAlH$_4$ reduction keeps all the carbon atoms of the amide.\nThe loss of one carbon happens in Hoffmann bromamide degradation (Br$_2$/NaOH), not in LiAlH$_4$ reduction.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":89,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Methanamine reacts with benzoyl chloride to give HCl and an organic product. The organic product is:","image":"","options":["Benzamide","N-Methylbenzamide","N-Phenylethanamide","N-Methylbenzenesulphonamide"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Benzoylation replaces one H on the nitrogen by the C6H5CO– group.\nThe reaction is CH3NH2 + C6H5COCl → CH3NHCOC6H5 + HCl.\nThe product, CH3NHCOC6H5, is N-methylbenzamide.\nThe sulphonamide in option (4) would form only with benzenesulphonyl chloride, not benzoyl chloride.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":90,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following groups can be introduced directly in place of the $-N_2^+$ group of an arenediazonium salt, in a single nitrogen-displacement step described in NCERT?\n–F, –I, –CN, –$NO_2$, –OH, –H, –$CH_3$, –COOH","image":"","options":["4","5","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"–F: yes, via $HBF_4$ and heat. –I: yes, via KI.\n–CN: yes, via CuCN. –$NO_2$: yes, via $NaNO_2$/Cu with the fluoroborate.\n–OH: yes, by warming to 283 K. –H: yes, via $H_3PO_2$ or ethanol.\n–$CH_3$: no such displacement reaction exists.\n–COOH: not in one step. It requires –CN first, followed by hydrolysis.\nTotal = 6.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":91,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (reaction of aniline) with List-II (main result):\nList-I\n(a) Aniline + bromine water, room temperature\n(b) Anilinium hydrogensulphate heated at 453–473 K\n(c) Aniline + conc. $HNO_3$/conc. $H_2SO_4$ (no protection)\n(d) Aniline + $(CH_3CO)_2O$\nList-II\n(i) Acetanilide\n(ii) Sulphanilic acid\n(iii) 2,4,6-Tribromoaniline\n(iv) Tarry oxidation products along with o-, p- and m-nitroanilines","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Bromine water brominates all o/p positions, giving a white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromoaniline → (iii).\n(b) Heating anilinium hydrogensulphate at 453–473 K gives p-aminobenzenesulphonic acid (sulphanilic acid) → (ii).\n(c) Unprotected nitration gives tarry oxidation products plus a mixture that includes m-nitroaniline → (iv).\n(d) Acetylation with acetic anhydride gives acetanilide → (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":92,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Azo compounds such as p-aminoazobenzene are generally coloured and are used as dyes.\nReason (R): In azo compounds, two aromatic rings are joined through an –N=N– bond, which gives an extended conjugated system.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that azo products are often coloured and are used as dyes.\nR is true: the two aromatic rings are linked through –N=N–, which creates an extended conjugated system.\nThis extended conjugation is the reason the compounds are coloured, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":93,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Match the reagent/condition used on benzenediazonium chloride (List-I) with the main organic product (List-II).\nList-I:\n(A) CuCN/KCN\n(B) $HBF_4$, then heat\n(C) Cu powder/HBr\n(D) Warm aqueous solution to 283 K\nList-II:\n(i) Phenol\n(ii) Bromobenzene\n(iii) Fluorobenzene\n(iv) Benzonitrile","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) CuCN is a Sandmeyer reaction that introduces –CN, giving benzonitrile (iv).\n(B) Fluoroboric acid precipitates the fluoroborate, which on heating gives fluorobenzene (iii).\n(C) Cu powder with HBr is the Gattermann reaction, giving bromobenzene (ii).\n(D) Letting the solution warm to 283 K hydrolyses the salt to phenol (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":94,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"In aqueous solution, the pKb of N-ethylethanamine is 3.00 and that of benzenamine (aniline) is 9.38. By roughly what factor is the $K_b$ of N-ethylethanamine larger than that of aniline?","image":"","options":["$2.4 \\times 10^{6}$","6.38","3.13","$2.4 \\times 10^{5}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: pKb (diethylamine) = 3.00 and pKb (aniline) = 9.38.\nFormula: $K_b = 10^{-pK_b}$, so the ratio of the two $K_b$ values is $10^{(9.38 - 3.00)}$.\nSubstituting: the ratio is $10^{6.38} = 10^{0.38} \\times 10^{6} \\approx 2.4 \\times 10^{6}$.\nSo diethylamine is about $2.4 \\times 10^{6}$ times stronger as a base.\nThe difference 6.38 (option 2) is only the gap in pKb values, not the ratio of $K_b$ values.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":95,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Which pair of biologically active compounds both contain a secondary amino group and are used to increase blood pressure?","image":"","options":["Novocain and benadryl","Adrenaline and ephedrine","Benadryl and adrenaline","Ephedrine and novocain"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT names adrenaline and ephedrine as two compounds with a secondary amino group that are used to raise blood pressure.\nNovocain is a synthetic amino compound used as an anaesthetic in dentistry.\nBenadryl is an antihistaminic drug that contains a tertiary amino group.\nTherefore every pair that includes novocain or benadryl is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":96,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (reaction) with List-II (major product).\nList-I:\n(A) Aniline + bromine water, room temperature\n(B) Aniline + concentrated H$_2$SO$_4$ (on mixing)\n(C) Product of (B) heated with H$_2$SO$_4$ at 453–473 K\n(D) Acetanilide + conc. HNO$_3$/conc. H$_2$SO$_4$, followed by hydrolysis\nList-II:\n(i) p-Nitroaniline\n(ii) Anilinium hydrogensulphate\n(iii) 2,4,6-Tribromoaniline\n(iv) Sulphanilic acid","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-iii, B-ii, C-iv, D-i","A-i, B-ii, C-iv, D-iii","A-iii, B-ii, C-i, D-iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A)–(iii): Bromine water tribrominates the strongly activated ring and gives a white precipitate.\n(B)–(ii): Aniline acts as a base towards conc. H$_2$SO$_4$ and forms the salt anilinium hydrogensulphate.\n(C)–(iv): Heating this salt at 453–473 K gives p-aminobenzenesulphonic acid, called sulphanilic acid.\n(D)–(i): With –NH$_2$ protected as –NHCOCH$_3$, nitration is controlled and the para product is major. Hydrolysis then gives p-nitroaniline.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":97,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In aqueous solution, (C$_2$H$_5$)$_3$N is a stronger base than C$_2$H$_5$NH$_2$, whereas (CH$_3$)$_3$N is a weaker base than CH$_3$NH$_2$.\nReason (R): The basic strength of alkylamines in aqueous solution is decided solely by the +I effect of the alkyl groups.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The pKb values are 3.25 for (C$_2$H$_5$)$_3$N and 3.29 for C$_2$H$_5$NH$_2$, so the tertiary ethylamine is stronger. They are 4.22 for (CH$_3$)$_3$N and 3.38 for CH$_3$NH$_2$, so the tertiary methylamine is weaker.\nR is false. In water, basicity results from the interplay of the inductive effect, solvation of the cation by H-bonding, and steric hindrance. If the +I effect alone decided it, tertiary amines would always be the strongest bases.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":98,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the physical properties of diazonium salts is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Benzenediazonium chloride is a colourless crystalline solid that dissolves readily in water.","Benzenediazonium chloride is stable in the dry state, so it is usually prepared in advance and stored.","Benzenediazonium fluoroborate is insoluble in water.","Benzenediazonium fluoroborate is stable at room temperature."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: benzenediazonium chloride is a colourless, water-soluble crystalline solid.\n(2) Incorrect: it decomposes easily in the dry state. It is stable only in cold solution, so it is used immediately after preparation and not stored.\n(3) and (4) Correct: the fluoroborate is water-insoluble and stable at room temperature. This is why it precipitates out and can be isolated.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":99,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"4-Nitroaniline is subjected to the following sequence:\n(i) $NaNO_2$/HCl, 273 K; (ii) CuBr; (iii) Fe/HCl; (iv) $NaNO_2$/HCl, 273 K; (v) KI.\nWhat is the final product?","image":"","options":["1-Bromo-4-nitrobenzene","1-Bromo-4-iodobenzene","1-Iodo-4-nitrobenzene","4-Bromophenol"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Steps (i)–(ii): the –$NH_2$ group is diazotised and then replaced by Br (Sandmeyer), giving 1-bromo-4-nitrobenzene.\nStep (iii): Fe/HCl reduces –$NO_2$ to –$NH_2$, giving 4-bromoaniline.\nStep (iv): this new amino group is diazotised to 4-bromobenzenediazonium chloride.\nStep (v): KI replaces $-N_2^+$ by I, giving 1-bromo-4-iodobenzene.\nOption (1) stops after step (ii). Option (4) would need warming to 283 K instead of KI.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":100,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Phenylmethanamine ($C_6H_5CH_2NH_2$, $pK_b$ = 4.70) is a much stronger base than benzenamine ($C_6H_5NH_2$, $pK_b$ = 9.38).\nReason (R): In phenylmethanamine the –NH$_2$ group is attached to an sp$^3$ carbon, so the lone pair on nitrogen is not in conjugation with the benzene ring.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: a smaller $pK_b$ means a stronger base, and 4.70 is much less than 9.38.\nR is true: in benzylamine a CH$_2$ group separates nitrogen from the ring, so the lone pair cannot delocalise into the ring.\nR explains A: the lone pair stays localised and available for protonation. Benzylamine therefore behaves almost like an alkylamine, while aniline's lone pair is tied up in resonance.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":101,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Aniline is subjected to the following sequence of steps:\n(1) acetic anhydride;\n(2) $Br_2$ in acetic acid (one equivalent);\n(3) hydrolysis of the amide;\n(4) $NaNO_2$/HCl at 273–278 K;\n(5) CuCN.\nThe major final product is:","image":"","options":["2,4,6-Tribromobenzonitrile","4-Bromobenzonitrile","3-Bromobenzonitrile","4-Bromoaniline"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: Acetylation gives acetanilide. –NHCOCH3 is still an o/p director but only moderately activating.\nStep 2: Controlled bromination puts one Br mainly at the para position (ortho is sterically crowded), giving 4-bromoacetanilide.\nStep 3: Hydrolysis gives 4-bromoaniline.\nStep 4: Diazotisation gives 4-bromobenzenediazonium chloride.\nStep 5: The Sandmeyer reaction with CuCN replaces –N2+ by –CN, giving 4-bromobenzonitrile.\nTribromination would occur only without protection. 4-Bromoaniline is only an intermediate.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":102,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The –NH2 group in aniline is a powerful activating, ortho/para directing group.\n(ii) Aniline with bromine water at room temperature gives p-bromoaniline as the main product.\n(iii) The anilinium ion formed in strongly acidic medium is meta directing.\n(iv) In sulphonation, aniline first forms anilinium hydrogensulphate with conc. $H_2SO_4$.\n(v) In the presence of $AlCl_3$, the nitrogen of aniline becomes positively charged and acts as a strongly activating group.\n(vi) Nitration of acetanilide followed by hydrolysis gives o-nitroaniline as the major product.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: resonance puts high electron density at the o/p positions.\n(ii) Incorrect: bromine water gives 2,4,6-tribromoaniline. Monobromination requires protection.\n(iii) Correct: –NH3+ withdraws electrons and directs meta.\n(iv) Correct: this salt is heated at 453–473 K to give sulphanilic acid.\n(v) Incorrect: the positively charged nitrogen strongly deactivates the ring.\n(vi) Incorrect: the p-nitro derivative is the major product.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":103,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Using the pKb values for amines in aqueous solution, which of the following is the strongest base in water?","image":"","options":["Methanamine","N-Ethylethanamine","N,N-Dimethylmethanamine","Ethanamine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: a smaller pKb (larger Kb) means a stronger base.\nThe aqueous pKb values are: methanamine 3.38, N-ethylethanamine 3.00, N,N-dimethylmethanamine 4.22 and ethanamine 3.29.\nN-Ethylethanamine, $(C_2H_5)_2NH$, has the lowest pKb, so it is the strongest base.\nEthanamine (3.29) is the most tempting wrong option, but its pKb is still higher than 3.00.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":104,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When ethyl bromide is heated with ethanolic ammonia in a sealed tube at 373 K, a mixture of primary, secondary and tertiary amines and a quaternary ammonium salt is obtained.\nReason (R): The primary amine formed is itself a nucleophile and can attack further molecules of ethyl bromide.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Ammonolysis has the drawback of giving a mixture of amines and finally a quaternary salt.\nR is true. The amine formed has a lone pair on nitrogen, so it acts as a nucleophile. It attacks more alkyl halide, giving the 2° amine, then the 3° amine, then the quaternary salt.\nR therefore directly explains why the mixture forms.\nUsing a large excess of NH$_3$ makes the primary amine the major product.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":105,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Benzenediazonium salt is subjected separately to each of the following treatments:\n(i) its solution is allowed to warm up to 283 K\n(ii) it is treated with phenol in alkaline medium\n(iii) it is treated with ethanol\n(iv) its fluoroborate is heated with aqueous $NaNO_2$ in the presence of Cu\n(v) it is treated with aniline\n(vi) it is treated with $CuBr/HBr$\n(vii) it is treated with $H_3PO_2$ and water\nIn how many of these cases is nitrogen gas evolved?","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"$N_2$ escapes only in reactions that displace the diazo group. Coupling reactions retain the $-N=N-$ link.\n(i) Hydrolysis gives phenol + $N_2$. Evolved.\n(ii) Coupling gives p-hydroxyazobenzene. No $N_2$.\n(iii) Reduction gives benzene, and ethanol is oxidised to ethanal. Evolved.\n(iv) The $-NO_2$ group replaces the diazo group, giving nitrobenzene. Evolved.\n(v) Coupling gives p-aminoazobenzene. No $N_2$.\n(vi) Sandmeyer reaction gives bromobenzene. Evolved.\n(vii) Reduction gives benzene. Evolved.\nThat makes 5 cases in total.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":106,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (named reaction/test) with List-II (reagent used):\nList-I\n(A) Sandmeyer reaction\n(B) Gattermann reaction\n(C) Carbylamine reaction\n(D) Hinsberg test\nList-II\n(I) CHCl$_3$ and ethanolic KOH\n(II) Copper powder with HX\n(III) C$_6$H$_5$SO$_2$Cl\n(IV) Cu(I) salt such as Cu$_2$Cl$_2$/HCl","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The Sandmeyer reaction replaces the diazo group by Cl, Br or CN using a Cu(I) salt, so A-IV.\n(B) The Gattermann reaction uses copper powder with the halogen acid, so B-II.\n(C) The carbylamine (isocyanide) test uses CHCl$_3$ and ethanolic KOH, so C-I.\n(D) Hinsberg's reagent is benzenesulphonyl chloride, so D-III.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":107,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"An amine X has the molecular formula $C_3H_9N$. It does not give the carbylamine test. With benzenesulphonyl chloride it forms a solid that is insoluble in aqueous NaOH. X is:","image":"","options":["Propan-1-amine","Propan-2-amine","N,N-Dimethylmethanamine","N-Methylethanamine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A negative carbylamine test means X is not a primary amine. This rules out propan-1-amine and propan-2-amine.\nA sulphonamide forms, so X must have an N–H; this rules out the tertiary amine.\nThe product is insoluble in alkali, so it has no acidic N–H left. This matches a secondary amine.\nThe only secondary amine with formula $C_3H_9N$ is $CH_3NHC_2H_5$, N-methylethanamine.\nN,N-dimethylmethanamine does not react with the Hinsberg reagent at all.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":108,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Match the reagent in List-I, which acts on benzenediazonium chloride, with the main organic product in List-II.\nList-I\n(a) $CuCN/KCN$\n(b) $KI$\n(c) $H_3PO_2 + H_2O$\n(d) $HBF_4$, then heat\nList-II\n(i) Fluorobenzene\n(ii) Benzene\n(iii) Iodobenzene\n(iv) Benzonitrile","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Cu(I) cyanide (Sandmeyer reaction) replaces $N_2^+$ by $-CN$, giving benzonitrile, so (a)-(iv).\n(b) Potassium iodide gives iodobenzene directly, so (b)-(iii).\n(c) Hypophosphorous acid is a mild reducing agent that replaces the diazo group by H, giving benzene, so (c)-(ii).\n(d) $HBF_4$ precipitates the diazonium fluoroborate, which gives fluorobenzene on heating, so (d)-(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":109,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"In the gaseous phase, where solvation effects are absent, the correct decreasing order of basic strength is:","image":"","options":["$(C_2H_5)_2NH > (C_2H_5)_3N > C_2H_5NH_2 > NH_3$","$(C_2H_5)_3N > (C_2H_5)_2NH > C_2H_5NH_2 > NH_3$","$C_2H_5NH_2 > (C_2H_5)_2NH > (C_2H_5)_3N > NH_3$","$NH_3 > C_2H_5NH_2 > (C_2H_5)_2NH > (C_2H_5)_3N$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In the gas phase, basicity depends only on the +I effect of the alkyl groups.\nMore alkyl groups push more electron density onto N, so the order is 3° > 2° > 1° > $NH_3$.\nOption (1) is the order in aqueous solution, where solvation and steric effects also act. It is not the gas-phase order.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":110,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"2.95 g of propan-1-amine ($C_3H_9N$, molar mass 59 g/mol) is treated with excess nitrous acid. Nitrogen is evolved quantitatively. What volume of $N_2$ is collected at STP? (Molar volume at STP = 22.4 L)","image":"","options":["2.24 L","1.12 L","0.56 L","3.36 L"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: mass = 2.95 g, M = 59 g/mol. A primary aliphatic amine gives one $N_2$ per molecule: $RNH_2 + HNO_2 \\rightarrow ROH + N_2 + H_2O$.\nFormula: moles of $N_2$ = moles of amine = $\\frac{2.95}{59}$ = 0.05 mol.\nSubstituting: V = 0.05 × 22.4 L.\nSo V = 1.12 L.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":111,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"An aromatic compound X (C7H7NO) on warming with bromine and aqueous NaOH gives Y (C6H7N). Y gives a foul smell when heated with CHCl3 and ethanolic KOH. Y is treated with NaNO2 and HCl at 273–278 K, and the product is then treated with hypophosphorous acid (H3PO2). What is the final organic product Z?","image":"","options":["Phenol","Toluene","Benzene","Chlorobenzene"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Step 1: X loses one carbon in Hoffmann bromamide degradation (C7 to C6), so X is benzamide, C6H5CONH2.\nStep 2: Y is aniline, C6H5NH2. As a primary amine, it gives the positive carbylamine test.\nStep 3: Diazotisation at 273–278 K converts aniline into benzenediazonium chloride.\nStep 4: H3PO2, a mild reducing agent, replaces –N2⁺ by H, giving benzene.\nPhenol would form only on warming in water, which is not done here.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":112,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Reduction of nitrobenzene with iron scrap and hydrochloric acid is preferred over other metal/acid reductions. The main reason is that:","image":"","options":["Iron converts the nitro group directly into an amide, which then hydrolyses to aniline","The FeCl$_2$ formed is hydrolysed and releases HCl, so only a small amount of acid is needed to start the reaction","Iron acts only as a catalyst and is fully regenerated at the end of the reaction","Fe/HCl gives a mixture of primary, secondary and tertiary amines, which is easy to separate"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: in the Fe/HCl reduction, the FeCl$_2$ formed gets hydrolysed and releases hydrochloric acid during the reaction.\nSo the acid is partly regenerated, and only a small amount of HCl is needed to start the reaction.\nIron is consumed as a reducing agent, not regenerated as a catalyst, so option (3) is wrong.\nNitro reduction gives only the primary amine, never a mixture of amines.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":113,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Aniline is shaken with bromine water at room temperature. What is the product?","image":"","options":["p-Bromoaniline as a yellow liquid","o-Bromoaniline as a colourless oil","2,4,6-Tribromoaniline as a white precipitate","3-Bromoaniline as a white precipitate"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The –NH2 group strongly activates the ring and directs incoming groups to the ortho and para positions.\nWith bromine water at room temperature, substitution cannot be stopped at one step. All three o/p positions (2, 4 and 6) are brominated.\nThe product separates as a white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromoaniline.\nMonobromination (o- or p-bromoaniline) needs the –NH2 group to be protected first. Meta substitution is not favoured by –NH2.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":114,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (conversion of benzenediazonium chloride) with List-II (reagent or product).\nList-I\nA. Sandmeyer reaction giving chlorobenzene\nB. Gattermann reaction giving chlorobenzene\nC. Replacement of the diazo group by H\nD. Coupling reaction giving p-hydroxyazobenzene\nList-II\n(i) $H_3PO_2$ (phosphinic acid)\n(ii) Copper powder + HCl\n(iii) Phenol\n(iv) $Cu_2Cl_2$/HCl","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A: The Sandmeyer reaction introduces Cl using a Cu(I) salt, here $Cu_2Cl_2$/HCl, so A-(iv).\nB: The Gattermann reaction uses copper powder with the halogen acid, so B-(ii).\nC: Mild reducing agents such as $H_3PO_2$ replace the diazo group by H to give benzene, so C-(i).\nD: Phenol couples at its para position to give p-hydroxyazobenzene, so D-(iii).\nThe tempting swap of A and B fails because Cu(I) belongs to Sandmeyer and Cu powder to Gattermann.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":115,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Consider substituents placed at the para position of aniline: –OCH3, –CH3, –NO2, –COOH, –Cl, –SO3H. How many of these increase the basic strength compared to aniline?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Electron-releasing groups make the nitrogen lone pair more available for protonation, so they increase basicity. –OCH3 and –CH3 are of this type.\nElectron-withdrawing groups decrease basicity. –NO2, –COOH, –SO3H and halogens such as –Cl are of this type.\nSo only 2 of the six substituents increase basic strength.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":116,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: An amine dissolved in ether can be extracted into aqueous HCl, and the amine is recovered by adding NaOH to the aqueous layer.\nStatement II: Amine salts are soluble in water but insoluble in organic solvents like ether.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. HCl converts the amine into its ammonium salt, which moves into the water layer. NaOH then regenerates the free amine.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT states that amine salts are water soluble but insoluble in ether.\nThis difference in solubility is the basis for separating amines from non-basic organic compounds.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":117,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"An ether solution contains an aliphatic amine and a neutral, water-insoluble organic compound. The solution is shaken with dilute HCl and the layers are separated. Which statement correctly describes how the amine is recovered?","image":"","options":["The amine stays in the ether layer and is recovered by evaporating the ether","The amine passes into the aqueous layer as its ammonium salt and is regenerated by adding NaOH","The amine passes into the aqueous layer and is regenerated by adding more HCl","The amine forms an insoluble salt that floats between the two layers"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Being basic, the amine reacts with HCl to form a substituted ammonium chloride.\nAmine salts are soluble in water but insoluble in ether, so the salt moves into the aqueous layer. The non-basic compound stays in the ether.\nAdding a base such as NaOH to the aqueous layer regenerates the free amine.\nAdding more acid would only keep the amine in its salt form.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":118,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Acylation of amines with acid chlorides is carried out in the presence of pyridine.\nStatement II: Pyridine removes the HCl formed and shifts the equilibrium towards the amide.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT says acylation is carried out in the presence of a base stronger than the amine, such as pyridine.\nStatement II is correct. Pyridine takes up the HCl produced, which drives the equilibrium to the right.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":119,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following compounds give a foul-smelling isocyanide when heated with chloroform and ethanolic KOH?\nMethanamine, aniline, N-methylmethanamine, N,N-dimethylaniline, phenylmethanamine, N-methylaniline, ethanamine","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The carbylamine (isocyanide) test is given only by primary amines, both aliphatic and aromatic.\nPrimary amines in the list: methanamine, aniline, phenylmethanamine (benzylamine) and ethanamine, which makes 4.\nN-methylmethanamine and N-methylaniline are secondary amines, so they do not respond.\nN,N-dimethylaniline is a tertiary amine, so it does not respond either.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":120,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Which option gives the correct increasing order of basic strength?","image":"","options":["p-Toluidine < aniline < p-nitroaniline","Aniline < p-nitroaniline < p-toluidine","p-Nitroaniline < aniline < p-toluidine","p-Nitroaniline < p-toluidine < aniline"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The –NO2 group withdraws electrons and lowers the electron density on nitrogen, so p-nitroaniline is the weakest base.\nThe –CH3 group releases electrons and raises the electron density on nitrogen, so p-toluidine is stronger than aniline.\nThe increasing order is therefore p-nitroaniline < aniline < p-toluidine.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":121,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"The $pK_b$ of aniline (9.38) is much higher than that of ammonia (4.75). Which of the following best explains why aniline is the weaker base?","image":"","options":["The lone pair on nitrogen is in conjugation with the benzene ring, so it is less available for protonation.","The phenyl group releases electrons by +I effect, which destabilises the anilinium ion.","Nitrogen in aniline is sp hybridised, so it holds its lone pair more tightly.","Aniline forms stronger hydrogen bonds with water than ammonia does, so the free base is stabilised."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In aniline the –NH$_2$ group is attached directly to the benzene ring.\nIts unshared electron pair is delocalised into the ring by resonance (conjugation), so it is less available to accept a proton. This makes aniline a weaker base than NH$_3$.\nOption (2) fails because NCERT describes the aryl group as electron withdrawing, not electron releasing.\nOption (3) fails because nitrogen in amines is sp$^3$ hybridised.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":122,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Four compounds of nearly equal molar mass (72–74 g/mol) are: n-butylamine, diethylamine, N,N-dimethylethanamine and butan-1-ol. Which one has the highest boiling point?","image":"","options":["n-Butylamine","Diethylamine","N,N-Dimethylethanamine","Butan-1-ol"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Boiling points of these compounds depend on intermolecular hydrogen bonding.\nOxygen (electronegativity 3.5) is more electronegative than nitrogen (3.0). So alcohols are more polar and form stronger hydrogen bonds than amines.\nThis gives butan-1-ol the highest boiling point, about 390 K.\nn-Butylamine (about 351 K) is the highest among the amines, but it is still well below butan-1-ol.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":123,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"The boiling points of three isomeric amines of molar mass 73 are: n-butylamine 350.8 K, diethylamine 329.3 K and ethyldimethylamine 310.5 K. What is the main reason that ethyldimethylamine has the lowest boiling point?","image":"","options":["It has the lowest molar mass of the three","It has no hydrogen on nitrogen, so it cannot form intermolecular hydrogen bonds","Its nitrogen is $sp^2$ hybridised, which makes the molecule planar","Its alkyl groups have the weakest +I effect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"All three amines are isomers with molar mass 73, so mass cannot explain the difference.\nPrimary amines have two N–H hydrogens and secondary amines have one, so both associate through intermolecular H-bonds.\nEthyldimethylamine is a tertiary amine with no H on nitrogen, so it has no intermolecular H-bonding.\nThis gives the order primary > secondary > tertiary for boiling points.\nThe nitrogen in amines is $sp^3$ hybridised, not $sp^2$, so option (3) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":124,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"9.3 g of aniline is treated with excess bromine water, and the reaction goes to completion. What mass of 2,4,6-tribromoaniline is precipitated? (Atomic masses: C = 12, H = 1, N = 14, Br = 80)","image":"","options":["9.3 g","17.2 g","24.9 g","33.0 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: mass of aniline (C6H5NH2) = 9.3 g; molar mass = 93 g/mol.\nFormula: C6H5NH2 + 3Br2 → C6H2Br3NH2 + 3HBr, so 1 mol aniline gives 1 mol product.\nMolar mass of C6H4Br3N = 72 + 4 + 240 + 14 = 330 g/mol.\nSubstituting: moles of aniline = 9.3/93 = 0.1 mol; mass of product = 0.1 × 330 g.\nSo mass of 2,4,6-tribromoaniline = 33.0 g.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":125,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the reaction of amines with benzenesulphonyl chloride is correct?","image":"","options":["Tertiary amines form sulphonamides that are insoluble in alkali","The sulphonamide from a primary amine dissolves in alkali because its N–H hydrogen is made acidic by the sulphonyl group","The sulphonamide formed from a secondary amine is soluble in aqueous alkali","Hinsberg's reagent is p-toluenesulphonyl chloride itself"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: tertiary amines do not react with benzenesulphonyl chloride at all.\n(2) Correct: the strongly electron-withdrawing –SO2– group makes the remaining N–H acidic, so the product from a primary amine dissolves in alkali.\n(3) Incorrect: the product from a secondary amine, such as the N,N-dialkyl sulphonamide, has no N–H. It is not acidic and stays insoluble in alkali.\n(4) Incorrect: Hinsberg's reagent is benzenesulphonyl chloride. p-Toluenesulphonyl chloride is the reagent that replaces it nowadays.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":126,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following reagents can convert nitrobenzene into aniline?\nH$_2$/Ni, H$_2$/Pd, H$_2$/Pt, Fe/HCl, Sn/HCl, aqueous NaOH, ethanolic NH$_3$","image":"","options":["3","4","5","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Catalytic hydrogenation with finely divided Ni, Pd or Pt reduces the nitro group to an amino group. That gives 3 reagents.\nMetals in acidic medium (Fe/HCl and Sn/HCl) also reduce nitro compounds to amines. That adds 2 more.\nAqueous NaOH is a base, not a reducing agent.\nEthanolic NH$_3$ is used for ammonolysis of halides and does not reduce –NO$_2$.\nTotal = 5.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":127,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Which reagent sequence converts 4-nitrotoluene into 2-bromobenzoic acid as the major product?","image":"","options":["$Br_2$/$FeBr_3$; Sn/HCl; $NaNO_2$/HCl at 273 K; $H_3PO_2$; $KMnO_4$/KOH then $H_3O^+$","$KMnO_4$/KOH then $H_3O^+$; $Br_2$/$FeBr_3$; Sn/HCl; $NaNO_2$/HCl at 273 K; $H_3PO_2$","Sn/HCl; $NaNO_2$/HCl at 273 K; $H_3PO_2$; $Br_2$/$FeBr_3$; $KMnO_4$/KOH then $H_3O^+$","$Br_2$/$FeBr_3$; Sn/HCl; $NaNO_2$/HCl at 273 K; warm to 283 K; $KMnO_4$/KOH then $H_3O^+$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: Brominate first. The –CH₃ group (ortho director) and the –NO₂ group (meta director) both send Br to C-2, giving 2-bromo-4-nitrotoluene.\nStep 2: Reduce –NO₂ to –NH₂, then diazotise and remove the group with $H_3PO_2$. This gives 2-bromotoluene.\nStep 3: $KMnO_4$ oxidises –CH₃ to –COOH, giving 2-bromobenzoic acid.\n(2) Oxidising first creates –COOH, which misdirects the Br.\n(3) Brominating toluene gives mainly the para product.\n(4) Warming introduces –OH.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":128,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the basicity of aliphatic amines are correct?\n(i) The larger a substituted ammonium ion, the less it is stabilised by solvation in water.\n(ii) Considering solvation alone, the expected order of basicity is primary > secondary > tertiary.\n(iii) A $-CH_3$ group causes no steric hindrance to H-bonding, but bulkier groups such as $-C_2H_5$ do.\n(iv) In water, trimethylamine is a weaker base than ammonia.\n(v) The +I effect of alkyl groups stabilises the substituted ammonium ion by dispersing its positive charge.\n(vi) In the gas phase, trimethylamine is more basic than dimethylamine.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: a larger ion is less solvated and therefore less stabilised.\n(ii) Correct: this order is opposite to the order predicted by the inductive effect.\n(iii) Correct: as stated in NCERT.\n(iv) Incorrect: the pKb of $(CH_3)_3N$ is 4.22, lower than 4.75 for $NH_3$, so trimethylamine is the stronger base.\n(v) Correct.\n(vi) Correct: in the gas phase the order is 3° > 2°.\nFive statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":129,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following in increasing order of pKb in aqueous solution:\n$NH_3$, $(CH_3)_3N$, $(C_2H_5)_3N$, $CH_3NH_2$","image":"","options":["$(CH_3)_3N < (C_2H_5)_3N < CH_3NH_2 < NH_3$","$(C_2H_5)_3N < CH_3NH_2 < (CH_3)_3N < NH_3$","$CH_3NH_2 < (C_2H_5)_3N < NH_3 < (CH_3)_3N$","$NH_3 < (CH_3)_3N < CH_3NH_2 < (C_2H_5)_3N$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The pKb values are: $(C_2H_5)_3N$ 3.25, $CH_3NH_2$ 3.38, $(CH_3)_3N$ 4.22 and $NH_3$ 4.75.\nIn increasing order: 3.25 < 3.38 < 4.22 < 4.75, which matches option (2).\nTertiary amines behave differently in the two series. In the ethyl series the tertiary amine is quite basic, but trimethylamine is poorly solvated and so has a higher pKb.\nAll aliphatic amines (pKb from 3 to 4.22) are still stronger bases than $NH_3$, so option (3) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":130,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about protecting the –NH$_2$ group of aniline by acetylation are correct?\n(i) In acetanilide, the lone pair on nitrogen is delocalised towards the carbonyl oxygen, so –NHCOCH$_3$ activates the ring less than –NH$_2$.\n(ii) The –NHCOCH$_3$ group is still an ortho/para director.\n(iii) Brominating acetanilide and then hydrolysing the product gives p-bromoaniline as the major product.\n(iv) Acetylation is carried out with acetic anhydride, and the free amino group is regenerated by hydrolysis of the amide.\n(v) Acetylation converts the amino group into a meta-directing group, and this is why nitration becomes controlled.\n(vi) Like acetanilide, aniline itself gives mainly a monobromo product with bromine water at room temperature.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: resonance of the N lone pair with C=O makes it less available to the ring.\n(ii) Correct: the lone pair is only partly withdrawn, so the group still directs o/p.\n(iii) Correct: moderated activation gives the monobromo (para) amide, and hydrolysis then gives p-bromoaniline.\n(iv) Correct: this is the protect–substitute–deprotect sequence.\n(v) Incorrect: –NHCOCH$_3$ is o/p directing, and the p-nitro product is major.\n(vi) Incorrect: aniline gives 2,4,6-tribromoaniline.\nCorrect statements = 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":131,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Butan-1-ol (b.p. 390.3 K) has a higher boiling point than butan-1-amine (b.p. 350.8 K), although their molar masses are nearly equal.\nReason (R): Oxygen (electronegativity 3.5) is more electronegative than nitrogen (3.0), so the O–H···O hydrogen bonds in alcohols are stronger than the N–H···N hydrogen bonds in amines.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Table 9.2 lists 390.3 K for n-butanol and 350.8 K for n-butylamine.\nR is true. Oxygen is more electronegative than nitrogen, so the O–H bond is more polar and alcohols form stronger intermolecular hydrogen bonds.\nStronger hydrogen bonding needs more energy to separate the molecules, which raises the boiling point. So R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":132,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Hinsberg's reagent, used to distinguish primary, secondary and tertiary amines, is:","image":"","options":["Benzoyl chloride","Benzenesulphonyl chloride","Acetic anhydride","Benzenediazonium chloride"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: benzenesulphonyl chloride, C6H5SO2Cl, is called Hinsberg's reagent.\nIt reacts with primary and secondary amines to form sulphonamides. Tertiary amines do not react with it.\nIn modern practice it has been replaced by p-toluenesulphonyl chloride.\nBenzoyl chloride is the reagent for benzoylation, not the Hinsberg test, so option (1) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":133,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Direct nitration of aniline with a mixture of conc. $HNO_3$ and conc. $H_2SO_4$ gives a substantial amount of m-nitroaniline.\nReason (R): In the strongly acidic medium, aniline is protonated to the anilinium ion, which is meta directing.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that direct nitration of aniline gives tarry oxidation products. Besides the o- and p-isomers, a significant amount of the meta derivative is also formed.\nR is true: in strongly acidic medium, aniline is protonated to C6H5NH3+. This group has no free lone pair, withdraws electrons and directs meta.\nThe meta product arises precisely because of this anilinium ion, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":134,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"The pKb of N-ethylethanamine is 3.00 and that of ammonia is 4.75 at the same temperature. By what factor is the $K_b$ of N-ethylethanamine greater than that of ammonia? (Take $10^{0.75} = 5.62$)","image":"","options":["1.75","56.2","0.58","5.62"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: pKb of $(C_2H_5)_2NH$ = 3.00 and pKb of $NH_3$ = 4.75.\nFormula: $K_b = 10^{-pK_b}$, so the ratio is $\\frac{K_{b1}}{K_{b2}} = 10^{(pK_{b2} - pK_{b1})}$.\nSubstituting: ratio = $10^{4.75 - 3.00} = 10^{1.75} = 10 \\times 5.62$.\nSo the ratio is about 56.2.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":135,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about $K_b$ and $pK_b$ of amines is correct?","image":"","options":["A larger value of $K_b$ corresponds to a larger value of $pK_b$","An amine with $pK_b$ = 3.0 is a stronger base than an amine with $pK_b$ = 4.0","The expression for $K_b$ has $[H_2O]$ in the denominator along with $[RNH_2]$","$pK_b$ is defined as $\\log K_b$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"$pK_b = -\\log K_b$. So a larger $K_b$ means a smaller $pK_b$, and options (1) and (4) are wrong.\n$K_b = \\frac{[RNH_3^+][OH^-]}{[RNH_2]}$. The nearly constant $[H_2O]$ is absorbed into the constant, so option (3) is wrong.\nA lower $pK_b$ (3.0) means a larger $K_b$, which means a stronger base. Option (2) is correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":136,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Ethanenitrile (CH$_3$CN) is reduced with LiAlH$_4$. The product formed is:","image":"","options":["Methanamine","Ethanamine","N-Methylmethanamine","Ethanamide"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"LiAlH$_4$ (or catalytic hydrogenation) reduces a nitrile to a primary amine: R–C≡N → R–CH$_2$NH$_2$.\nThe nitrile carbon becomes the CH$_2$ group, so no carbon is lost. CH$_3$CN therefore gives CH$_3$CH$_2$NH$_2$, which is ethanamine.\nMethanamine would require the loss of one carbon.\nThe product is a primary amine, so N-methylmethanamine (a secondary amine) is ruled out. Ethanamide is a hydrolysis product, not a reduction product.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":137,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (conversion) with List-II (reagent/condition).\nList-I\n(A) CH$_3$CH$_2$Cl → CH$_3$CH$_2$CH$_2$NH$_2$\n(B) CH$_3$CH$_2$Cl → CH$_3$CH$_2$NH$_2$\n(C) CH$_3$CH$_2$CONH$_2$ → CH$_3$CH$_2$CH$_2$NH$_2$\n(D) CH$_3$CH$_2$NO$_2$ → CH$_3$CH$_2$NH$_2$\nList-II\n(I) Large excess of ethanolic NH$_3$, sealed tube, 373 K\n(II) H$_2$ with finely divided Ni\n(III) Alcoholic KCN, then LiAlH$_4$\n(IV) LiAlH$_4$ followed by H$_2$O","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The product has one more carbon. CN$^-$ replaces Cl to form a nitrile, and LiAlH$_4$ then reduces it to the amine (ascent of the series): III.\n(B) The product has the same number of carbons. Ammonolysis with excess NH$_3$ makes the 1° amine the major product: I.\n(C) LiAlH$_4$ reduces the amide to an amine with the same carbon count: IV.\n(D) Nitroalkanes are reduced by H$_2$/Ni to alkanamines: II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":138,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"24.6 g of nitrobenzene is reduced with Fe/HCl. The aniline is then liberated and isolated with an overall yield of 80%. What mass of aniline is obtained? (Atomic masses: C = 12, H = 1, N = 14, O = 16)","image":"","options":["18.6 g","14.88 g","11.9 g","23.25 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: 24.6 g of C$_6$H$_5$NO$_2$, yield = 80%.\nMolar masses: C$_6$H$_5$NO$_2$ = 72 + 5 + 14 + 32 = 123 g/mol; C$_6$H$_5$NH$_2$ = 72 + 7 + 14 = 93 g/mol.\nMoles of nitrobenzene = 24.6/123 = 0.2 mol. The mole ratio of nitrobenzene to aniline is 1 : 1.\nTheoretical mass of aniline = 0.2 × 93 = 18.6 g.\nActual mass = 0.80 × 18.6 = 14.88 g.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":139,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Benzenediazonium chloride is warmed with ethanol, and benzene is formed. What is ethanol converted into in this reaction?","image":"","options":["Ethanoic acid","Ethanal","Ethene","Chloroethane"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Ethanol acts as a mild reducing agent. It replaces the $-N_2^+$ group by H and gives the arene (benzene).\nIn doing so, ethanol is itself oxidised to ethanal ($CH_3CHO$).\nWith hypophosphorous acid as the reducing agent, the oxidation product is phosphorous acid instead.\nEthanoic acid would need further oxidation, which does not occur here.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":140,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Arenediazonium salts can be kept for a short time in cold aqueous solution, whereas alkyldiazonium salts decompose almost as soon as they form.\nReason (R): In the arenediazonium ion, the positive charge is delocalised over the benzene ring by resonance.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Primary aliphatic amines give highly unstable alkyldiazonium salts, which at once lose $N_2$ to form alcohols. Arenediazonium salts survive for a short time at 273–278 K.\nR is true. The $-N_2^+$ group is conjugated with the benzene ring, so the positive charge is spread through resonance.\nThis resonance is exactly why the aryl ion is more stable. An alkyl group cannot delocalise the charge in this way.\nSo R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":141,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following amines, on heating with chloroform and ethanolic potassium hydroxide, gives a foul-smelling isocyanide?","image":"","options":["N-Methylaniline","N,N-Dimethylaniline","Aniline","Triethylamine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The carbylamine (isocyanide) test is given only by primary amines, both aliphatic and aromatic.\nAniline, $C_6H_5NH_2$, is a primary arylamine. It gives phenyl isocyanide, $C_6H_5NC$, which has a foul smell.\nN-Methylaniline is a secondary amine, so it does not respond.\nN,N-Dimethylaniline and triethylamine are tertiary amines, so they do not respond either.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":142,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following give an alkali-soluble product on reaction with benzenesulphonyl chloride?\nEthanamine, N-ethylethanamine, N,N-diethylethanamine, aniline, N-methylaniline, phenylmethanamine, N,N-dimethylaniline, propan-2-amine","image":"","options":["3","5","4","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Only primary amines give an alkali-soluble sulphonamide, because the product still has an acidic N–H.\nPrimary: ethanamine, aniline, phenylmethanamine and propan-2-amine. That makes 4.\nSecondary: N-ethylethanamine and N-methylaniline form sulphonamides with no N–H, which are insoluble in alkali.\nTertiary: N,N-diethylethanamine and N,N-dimethylaniline do not react at all.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":143,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is the strongest base in aqueous solution?","image":"","options":["Benzenamine","N-Methylaniline","Phenylmethanamine","4-Nitroaniline"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In phenylmethanamine (benzylamine, C6H5CH2NH2), a CH2 group separates the –NH2 group from the ring.\nIts lone pair is therefore not in conjugation with the ring, and its pKb is 4.70.\nIn the other three compounds nitrogen is attached directly to the ring, so they are weak bases. Their pKb values are about 9 or higher.\n4-Nitroaniline is the weakest of all because the –NO2 group withdraws electrons.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":144,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In aqueous solution, trimethylamine is a weaker base than methylamine.\nReason (R): The trimethylammonium ion has only one N–H hydrogen, so it is the least stabilised by hydrogen bonding with water.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The pKb of $(CH_3)_3N$ is 4.22, which is higher than 3.38 for $CH_3NH_2$, so trimethylamine is the weaker base in water.\nR is true. $(CH_3)_3NH^+$ can form only one H-bond with water, while $CH_3NH_3^+$ can form three. Fewer H-bonds mean less stabilisation by solvation.\nA less stabilised conjugate acid means a weaker base, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":145,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Introducing a –CH$_3$ group at the para position of aniline increases its basic strength.\nStatement II: Aniline is a weaker base than ammonia because aniline is more stabilised by resonance relative to the anilinium ion.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. –CH3 is electron-releasing and increases the electron density on nitrogen, so p-toluidine is more basic than aniline.\nStatement II is correct. Aniline has five resonance structures and the anilinium ion has only two.\nThe free base is therefore stabilised more than its conjugate acid, which disfavours protonation and makes aniline weaker than ammonia.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":146,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Ammonolysis of an alkyl halide generally gives a mixture of primary, secondary and tertiary amines along with a quaternary ammonium salt.\nReason (R): The primary amine formed first is itself a nucleophile and can react further with the alkyl halide.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT names this mixture as the main disadvantage of ammonolysis.\nR is true: the primary amine, like ammonia, has a lone pair on nitrogen and acts as a nucleophile.\nThe 1° amine attacks more alkyl halide to give a 2° amine, which reacts again to give a 3° amine and finally the quaternary salt.\nSo R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":147,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"For the equilibrium $RNH_2 + H_2O \\rightleftharpoons RNH_3^+ + OH^-$, which expression correctly defines the base dissociation constant $K_b$?","image":"","options":["$K_b = \\frac{[RNH_2]}{[RNH_3^+][OH^-]}$","$K_b = \\frac{[RNH_3^+][OH^-]}{[RNH_2][H_2O]}$","$K_b = \\frac{[RNH_3^+][OH^-]}{[RNH_2]}$","$K_b = \\frac{[RNH_3^+]}{[RNH_2][OH^-]}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Start from the equilibrium constant: $K = \\frac{[RNH_3^+][OH^-]}{[RNH_2][H_2O]}$.\nWater is present in large excess, so $[H_2O]$ is effectively constant and is absorbed into K.\nThis gives $K_b = K[H_2O] = \\frac{[RNH_3^+][OH^-]}{[RNH_2]}$.\nOption (2) is the plain K, not $K_b$. A larger $K_b$ (smaller pKb) means a stronger base.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":148,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Consider all the structural isomers of molecular formula C4H11N that are amines. How many of them are mixed amines, that is, secondary or tertiary amines in which the groups attached to nitrogen are not all the same?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Primary amines (4): butan-1-amine, butan-2-amine, 2-methylpropan-1-amine and 2-methylpropan-2-amine. The term simple/mixed does not apply to these.\nSecondary amines (3): N-methylpropan-1-amine (mixed), N-methylpropan-2-amine (mixed) and N-ethylethanamine (two ethyl groups, so simple).\nTertiary amine (1): N,N-dimethylethanamine, which has methyl and ethyl groups, so it is mixed.\nTotal mixed amines = 2 + 1 = 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":149,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Aniline is a much weaker base than ammonia. Which reason given in NCERT best explains this?","image":"","options":["The lone pair on nitrogen is in conjugation with the benzene ring, so it is less available for protonation","The phenyl group releases electrons to nitrogen by +I effect","Aniline forms stronger hydrogen bonds with water than ammonia does","Nitrogen in aniline is sp hybridised and holds its lone pair tightly"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In aniline the –NH2 group is bonded directly to the benzene ring.\nThe lone pair on nitrogen is delocalised into the ring by resonance, so it is less available to accept a proton.\nThe aryl group withdraws electrons. It does not release them, so option (2) has the effect reversed.\nThis gives aniline a pKb of 9.38, compared with 4.75 for ammonia.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":150,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Among isomeric amines, the tertiary amine has the lowest boiling point.\nReason (R): Tertiary amines have no hydrogen atom on nitrogen, so their molecules cannot associate through intermolecular hydrogen bonding.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT gives the boiling-point order of isomeric amines as primary > secondary > tertiary. For example, (C2H5)N(CH3)2 boils at 310.5 K, which is below both n-C4H9NH2 and (C2H5)2NH.\nR is true. A tertiary amine has no N–H bond, so its molecules cannot hydrogen bond with one another.\nR explains A, because weaker intermolecular forces mean a lower boiling point.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":151,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following compounds will give a foul-smelling product on heating with chloroform and ethanolic KOH?\nMethanamine, aniline, N-methylmethanamine, N,N-diethylethanamine, phenylmethanamine, N-methylaniline, propan-2-amine","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Only primary amines, whether aliphatic or aromatic, give the carbylamine (isocyanide) test.\nPrimary amines in the list: methanamine, aniline, phenylmethanamine (benzylamine) and propan-2-amine, which makes 4.\nNot primary: N-methylmethanamine and N-methylaniline are secondary, and N,N-diethylethanamine is tertiary. None of these three reacts.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":152,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In reducing nitrobenzene to aniline, iron scrap with hydrochloric acid is preferred because the FeCl$_2$ formed hydrolyses to release HCl, so only a little acid is needed to start the reaction.\nStatement II: In Hoffmann bromamide degradation, the amine formed contains one carbon atom more than the amide used.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. FeCl$_2$ is hydrolysed and regenerates HCl, so only a small amount of acid is needed to initiate the reaction.\nStatement II is incorrect. In Hoffmann degradation the alkyl or aryl group migrates from the carbonyl carbon to nitrogen, and the carbonyl carbon is lost. The amine therefore has one carbon fewer than the amide. Ascent of the series is done by nitrile reduction instead.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":153,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following primary amines can be prepared by Gabriel phthalimide synthesis using a suitable halide?\nMethanamine, ethanamine, aniline, phenylmethanamine (benzylamine), 4-bromoaniline, butan-1-amine","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Gabriel synthesis works with alkyl and benzyl halides. It fails with aryl halides because they do not undergo nucleophilic substitution with the phthalimide anion.\nMethanamine (from CH$_3$X): possible.\nEthanamine (from C$_2$H$_5$X): possible.\nButan-1-amine (from 1-halobutane): possible.\nBenzylamine (from benzyl chloride, where NH$_2$ sits on CH$_2$, not on the ring): possible.\nAniline and 4-bromoaniline need an aryl halide, so they are not possible.\nTotal = 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":154,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"An aromatic amide X (C$_7$H$_7$NO) is heated with Br$_2$ and aqueous NaOH to give Y. Y is diazotised with NaNO$_2$/HCl at 273–278 K, and the product is treated with CuCN to give Z. Z is reduced with LiAlH$_4$ to give W. Which statement about W is correct?","image":"","options":["W is aniline, which is a weaker base than ammonia","W is phenylmethanamine, a primary amine that is a much stronger base than aniline","W is N-methylaniline, a secondary amine","W is phenylmethanamine, which is a weaker base than ammonia"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: X is benzamide (C$_6$H$_5$CONH$_2$). Hoffmann degradation removes one carbon and gives Y = aniline.\nStep 2: Diazotisation followed by Sandmeyer reaction with CuCN gives Z = benzonitrile, C$_6$H$_5$CN.\nStep 3: LiAlH$_4$ reduces the nitrile to a primary amine, W = C$_6$H$_5$CH$_2$NH$_2$ (phenylmethanamine).\nIn W the –NH$_2$ is not attached to the ring, so its lone pair is not delocalised. Its pKb is 4.70, against 9.38 for aniline, so it is much more basic.\nOption (4) fails because 4.70 is less than 4.75 (ammonia), so W is slightly stronger than ammonia, not weaker.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":155,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"What is the correct order of reactivity of alkyl halides towards ammonia/amines in ammonolysis?","image":"","options":["RCl > RBr > RI","RBr > RI > RCl","RI > RCl > RBr","RI > RBr > RCl"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Ammonolysis is a nucleophilic substitution in which the C–X bond breaks.\nThe C–I bond is the weakest and the C–Cl bond is the strongest of the three, so iodides react fastest.\nNCERT gives the order RI > RBr > RCl.\nOption (1) reverses this order by wrongly relating reactivity to the electronegativity of the halogen.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-156","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":156,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following reaction sequences correctly converts methanamine (CH$_3$NH$_2$) into ethanamine (CH$_3$CH$_2$NH$_2$)?","image":"","options":["NaNO$_2$/HCl (cold); then PBr$_3$; then KCN (alc.); then LiAlH$_4$","NaNO$_2$/HCl (cold); then PBr$_3$; then excess ethanolic NH$_3$ at 373 K","Br$_2$/NaOH; then KCN (alc.); then LiAlH$_4$","NaNO$_2$/HCl (cold); then PBr$_3$; then KCN (alc.); then Br$_2$/NaOH"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Ascent of the series needs one extra carbon, and nitrile reduction provides it.\n(1) Correct. CH$_3$NH$_2$ with HNO$_2$ gives CH$_3$OH. PBr$_3$ then gives CH$_3$Br, and KCN gives CH$_3$CN. LiAlH$_4$ finally gives CH$_3$CH$_2$NH$_2$.\n(2) Ammonolysis of CH$_3$Br only gives back CH$_3$NH$_2$; no carbon is added.\n(3) Br$_2$/NaOH (Hoffmann) acts on amides, not on amines.\n(4) Br$_2$/NaOH acts on an amide, not a nitrile, and would also remove a carbon.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-157","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":157,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly shows the increasing order of basic strength in aqueous solution?","image":"","options":["C6H5CH2NH2 < C6H5NH2 < C6H5NHCH3 < C6H5N(CH3)2","C6H5NH2 < C6H5NHCH3 < C6H5N(CH3)2 < C6H5CH2NH2","C6H5N(CH3)2 < C6H5NHCH3 < C6H5NH2 < C6H5CH2NH2","C6H5NH2 < C6H5CH2NH2 < C6H5NHCH3 < C6H5N(CH3)2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In aniline and its N-methyl derivatives, the lone pair is in conjugation with the ring, so all three are weak bases. Methyl groups on N add a +I effect: pKb is 9.38 for aniline, 9.30 for N-methylaniline and 8.92 for N,N-dimethylaniline.\nIn phenylmethanamine, a CH2 group separates N from the ring. There is no resonance, so it is much more basic (pKb 4.70).\nThe increasing order is therefore aniline < N-methylaniline < N,N-dimethylaniline < benzylamine.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-158","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":158,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Amines behave as Lewis bases. Which feature of their structure is responsible for this?","image":"","options":["An unshared pair of electrons on the sp$^3$ hybridised nitrogen atom","Acidic hydrogen atoms attached to the nitrogen atom","A planar sp$^2$ hybridised nitrogen atom","Hydroxide ions present within the amine molecule"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In an amine, nitrogen is sp$^3$ hybridised. Three orbitals form bonds, and the fourth holds an unshared electron pair.\nA Lewis base is an electron-pair donor. The nitrogen donates this lone pair to a proton or another acid.\nN–H hydrogens do not make a molecule a Lewis base, and tertiary amines have none at all.\nNitrogen in amines is pyramidal, not planar sp$^2$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-159","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":159,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Benzylamine is a much stronger base than aniline.\nReason (R): In benzylamine the –NH$_2$ group is not attached directly to the benzene ring, so its lone pair is not delocalised into the ring.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The pKb of benzylamine is 4.70, while that of aniline is 9.38.\nR is true. The CH2 group in benzylamine cuts off conjugation, so the lone pair stays on nitrogen and remains available for a proton.\nIn aniline the lone pair is delocalised over the ring.\nThe difference in conjugation is exactly what makes benzylamine more basic, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-160","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":160,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Primary aromatic amines give diazonium salts that can be used in solution at 273–278 K, whereas primary aliphatic amines give diazonium salts that decompose at once.\nReason (R): In the arenediazonium ion, the positive charge is dispersed over the benzene ring by resonance.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Alkyldiazonium salts are highly unstable and lose $N_2$ immediately, giving alcohols. Arenediazonium salts survive for a short time in cold solution.\nR is true. NCERT explains the stability of the arenediazonium ion on the basis of resonance with the aromatic ring, which delocalises the charge.\nThis resonance is exactly why the aryl salt is more stable than the alkyl salt, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-161","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":161,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Which is the correct decreasing order of basic strength in aqueous solution?","image":"","options":["(C$_2$H$_5$)$_2$NH > C$_2$H$_5$NH$_2$ > NH$_3$ > C$_6$H$_5$CH$_2$NH$_2$ > C$_6$H$_5$NH$_2$","(C$_2$H$_5$)$_2$NH > C$_2$H$_5$NH$_2$ > C$_6$H$_5$CH$_2$NH$_2$ > NH$_3$ > C$_6$H$_5$NH$_2$","C$_2$H$_5$NH$_2$ > (C$_2$H$_5$)$_2$NH > C$_6$H$_5$CH$_2$NH$_2$ > C$_6$H$_5$NH$_2$ > NH$_3$","(C$_2$H$_5$)$_2$NH > C$_6$H$_5$CH$_2$NH$_2$ > C$_2$H$_5$NH$_2$ > NH$_3$ > C$_6$H$_5$NH$_2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The pKb values are: (C2H5)2NH 3.00, C2H5NH2 3.29, C6H5CH2NH2 4.70, NH3 4.75 and C6H5NH2 9.38.\nA lower pKb means a stronger base.\nBenzylamine, at 4.70, is slightly more basic than ammonia at 4.75. This rules out option (1).\nAniline, with its lone pair in conjugation with the ring, is the weakest base.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-162","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":162,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about converting aniline into 1,3,5-tribromobenzene are correct?\n(i) Aniline with bromine water at room temperature gives a white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromoaniline.\n(ii) The –NH₂ group is then removed by diazotisation followed by treatment with $H_3PO_2$ or ethanol.\n(iii) 1,3,5-Tribromobenzene is conveniently made by direct bromination of benzene because Br is a meta-directing group.\n(iv) The species reduced in the last step is a 2,4,6-tribromobenzenediazonium salt.\n(v) If ethanol is used in the last step, it is oxidised to ethanal.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: –NH₂ strongly activates the ring, so all ortho and para positions are brominated.\n(ii) Correct: diazotisation followed by reductive removal replaces N by H.\n(iii) Incorrect: Br is ortho/para directing, so direct bromination of benzene cannot give the 1,3,5-pattern.\n(iv) Correct: diazotising 2,4,6-tribromoaniline gives this diazonium salt.\n(v) Correct: ethanol is oxidised to ethanal while reducing the salt.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-163","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":163,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Which reagent is used to protect the –NH2 group of aniline so that its activating effect is reduced before electrophilic substitution?","image":"","options":["Chloroform and ethanolic KOH","Acetic anhydride","Sodium nitrite and HCl at 273–278 K","Benzenesulphonyl chloride in aqueous NaOH"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT: the activating effect of –NH2 is controlled by acetylating it with acetic anhydride, which gives acetanilide.\nAfter the desired substitution, the amide is hydrolysed to give back the substituted amine.\nCHCl3/alc. KOH gives the carbylamine (isocyanide) test.\nNaNO2/HCl at 273–278 K brings about diazotisation.\nBenzenesulphonyl chloride is the Hinsberg reagent, used to distinguish amines.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-164","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":164,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Aniline does not undergo Friedel–Crafts alkylation or acylation because:","image":"","options":["it forms a salt with $AlCl_3$, so the nitrogen becomes positively charged and strongly deactivates the ring","the –NH2 group is a meta director","the benzene ring of aniline has very low electron density","aniline is insoluble in the solvents used for this reaction"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Friedel–Crafts reactions use AlCl3, a Lewis acid, as the catalyst.\nThe basic nitrogen of aniline donates its lone pair to AlCl3 and forms a salt. The nitrogen therefore acquires a positive charge.\nThe positively charged group strongly deactivates the ring, so no substitution occurs.\nFree –NH2 is actually an o/p director and makes the ring electron-rich, so options (2) and (3) are wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-165","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":165,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following compounds react with benzenesulphonyl chloride to form a sulphonamide?\nEthanamine, N-ethylethanamine, N,N-diethylethanamine, aniline, N-methylaniline, N,N-dimethylaniline, phenylmethanamine","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Only amines with an H on nitrogen (primary and secondary) form sulphonamides.\nPrimary amines: ethanamine, aniline and phenylmethanamine. All three react.\nSecondary amines: N-ethylethanamine and N-methylaniline. Both react.\nTertiary amines: N,N-diethylethanamine and N,N-dimethylaniline. These do not react.\nTotal that react = 5.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-166","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":166,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Aniline is converted into benzenediazonium chloride (diazotisation) using which reagent and condition?","image":"","options":["$NaNO_2$ and $HCl$ at 273–278 K","$NaNO_2$ and $HCl$ at 373 K","Conc. $HNO_3$ and conc. $H_2SO_4$ at 323 K","$NaNO_2$ and $NaOH$ at 273–278 K"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key fact: nitrous acid is generated in situ from $NaNO_2$ and $HCl$. It converts aniline into benzenediazonium chloride at 273–278 K:\n$C_6H_5NH_2 + NaNO_2 + 2HCl \\rightarrow C_6H_5N_2^+Cl^- + NaCl + 2H_2O$\nThe low temperature is essential because the salt is unstable. At higher temperatures it decomposes, for example to phenol.\nOption (4) fails because without acid no $HNO_2$ forms. Option (3) is a nitrating mixture.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-167","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":167,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about diazonium salts is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Benzenediazonium chloride is a colourless crystalline solid that dissolves readily in water","Benzenediazonium fluoroborate is insoluble in water and stable at room temperature","Dry benzenediazonium chloride is stable and is usually stored for later use","The Sandmeyer reaction generally gives a better yield of chlorobenzene than the Gattermann reaction"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT describes benzenediazonium chloride as a colourless crystalline solid that is readily soluble in water.\n(2) Correct: the fluoroborate is water-insoluble and stable at room temperature, which is why it can be isolated.\n(3) Incorrect: the chloride decomposes easily in the dry state, and it reacts with water when warmed. It is therefore used immediately after preparation, not stored.\n(4) Correct: the Sandmeyer reaction (Cu(I) salts) gives a better yield than the Gattermann reaction (Cu powder with HX).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-168","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":168,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"In the industrial reduction of nitrobenzene to aniline, iron scrap and hydrochloric acid are preferred over other metal/acid combinations. What is the main reason?","image":"","options":["Iron is a stronger reducing agent than tin or zinc, so the reaction goes faster","The FeCl$_2$ formed is hydrolysed and releases HCl, so only a small amount of acid is needed to start the reaction","Fe/HCl reduces only the –NO$_2$ group and leaves the benzene ring untouched, unlike H$_2$/Ni","Fe/HCl gives the free amine directly, with no anilinium salt formed"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact (NCERT): Fe/HCl is preferred because the FeCl$_2$ formed gets hydrolysed and releases HCl during the reaction.\nSo the acid is regenerated, and only a small amount of HCl is needed to initiate the reaction. This makes the process economical.\nOption (1) is wrong because the preference is not based on reducing power.\nOption (4) is wrong because in acidic medium the amine is present as its salt.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-169","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":169,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following give a primary amine as the main organic product?\n(i) Propanamide + $Br_2$ + aqueous NaOH\n(ii) Potassium phthalimide + chlorobenzene, followed by alkaline hydrolysis\n(iii) Ethanenitrile + $LiAlH_4$\n(iv) Nitrobenzene + Fe scrap/HCl\n(v) Ethanamine + benzenesulphonyl chloride\n(vi) Aniline + $CHCl_3$ + ethanolic KOH, heated","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Yes. Hoffmann bromamide degradation gives ethanamine, which has one carbon less than the amide.\n(ii) No. Aryl halides do not undergo nucleophilic substitution with the phthalimide anion, so no amine forms.\n(iii) Yes. Reducing the nitrile gives ethanamine.\n(iv) Yes. Reduction with Fe/HCl gives aniline.\n(v) No. The product is a sulphonamide, N-ethylbenzenesulphonamide.\n(vi) No. The carbylamine reaction gives phenyl isocyanide.\nSo three reactions give a primary amine.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-170","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":170,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"Benzenediazonium chloride is treated separately with each of the following reagents/conditions:\nCuCN; KI; phenol (alkaline medium); H$_3$PO$_2$; aniline; ethanol; water warmed to 283 K; HBF$_4$ followed by heating.\nIn how many cases is nitrogen lost as N$_2$ gas from the diazonium group?","image":"","options":["4","5","6","8"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Displacement of nitrogen (N$_2$ escapes) occurs in six cases:\n- CuCN gives benzonitrile.\n- KI gives iodobenzene.\n- H$_3$PO$_2$ and ethanol each give benzene.\n- Warm water gives phenol.\n- HBF$_4$ followed by heating gives fluorobenzene.\nRetention of the diazo group occurs with phenol and aniline. These are coupling reactions that give p-hydroxyazobenzene and p-aminoazobenzene, which keep the –N=N– link.\nTotal = 6.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-AMINES-171","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Amines","qno":171,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Amines","type":"mcq","question":"According to the pKb values of amines in aqueous solution (NCERT Table 9.3), which of the following is the strongest base in water?","image":"","options":["Methanamine","N-Methylmethanamine","N-Ethylethanamine","N,N-Diethylethanamine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The smaller the pKb, the stronger the base.\nThe pKb values are: methanamine 3.38, N-methylmethanamine 3.27, N-ethylethanamine 3.00 and N,N-diethylethanamine 3.25.\nN-Ethylethanamine, $(C_2H_5)_2NH$, has the lowest pKb (3.00), so it is the strongest base.\nThe tempting choice is the tertiary amine, but in water it is weaker than the 2° amine because solvation and steric effects also act.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 9 · Amines","difficulty":"easy"}]});
